WEBVTT

00:16.072 --> 00:19.460
All right, we're good to go. I think

00:19.960 --> 00:23.233
we'll go ahead and get started right on 4 o'clock. So we'll start the work

00:23.733 --> 00:27.969
session. And the first item to talk about today is a discussion

00:28.469 --> 00:31.647
about the towns at Briley Farms Phase B, an update. And I

00:31.663 --> 00:34.307
believe Steven's got this, right?

00:35.194 --> 00:35.677
With Greg?

00:38.193 --> 00:41.412
Hopefully. Or we can just invite Greg

00:41.461 --> 00:47.209
up if you're— OK. Go ahead and tell

00:47.709 --> 00:51.910
us what you got. Let me plug in my— so you can see

00:52.410 --> 00:53.600
what I'm talking about here. Yep.

01:06.010 --> 01:09.582
Good deal. So first I wanted to say thank you.

01:09.970 --> 01:13.962
Last week at the City Council meeting, you approved the first

01:14.462 --> 01:17.831
amendment to the development agreement, and that allowed us to count the

01:18.331 --> 01:21.575
4 that were in lots from— or the

01:22.075 --> 01:24.518
4 lots that were in Plat 2 that we hadn't anticipated when we made the

01:24.534 --> 01:28.886
development agreement. Agree to the sequencing with parks

01:29.386 --> 01:32.797
and platform. So I appreciate that.

01:33.758 --> 01:37.461
I used to be on a planning commission, and one of my pet peeves

01:37.961 --> 01:41.115
was when people would come in and we'd give them recommendations

01:41.163 --> 01:44.352
or make suggestions, and it seemed like they would kind of do

01:44.368 --> 01:47.878
some token changes but didn't really listen to us. And when they came back,

01:47.894 --> 01:51.100
it was like, "Were you not taking notes?" Yeah.

01:51.565 --> 01:54.498
So we tried to take good notes from last time,

01:54.754 --> 01:58.253
and. Make some modifications with Element

01:58.285 --> 02:01.625
Homes as architectural partners, and we wanted to come and kind

02:01.657 --> 02:04.178
of run this by you and get your, your feedback.

02:04.788 --> 02:08.866
So last time when we were here, the mayor asked about the possibility

02:09.123 --> 02:12.254
of pushing the townhome back and extending the

02:12.754 --> 02:16.252
garage out 2 feet, and we actually

02:16.284 --> 02:20.668
did it about 3 feet. And so this new design

02:22.433 --> 02:25.552
is 24 feet deep on the garage.

02:26.347 --> 02:29.980
And we had the— our architectural partners put in some

02:30.480 --> 02:34.324
different trucks and minivans and sedans because

02:34.373 --> 02:38.150
I remember you asked about different cars and how

02:38.199 --> 02:41.952
they would fit. So this is a Ford F-150

02:42.709 --> 02:46.332
crew cab and it's parked in the

02:46.832 --> 02:49.930
garage. You can see that they put in garbage cans to scale. Yeah. And then

02:49.962 --> 02:53.354
they have some minivans. There's a Honda Odyssey and a Toyota

02:53.514 --> 02:57.902
Sienna and some different sedans like a Honda Accord

02:58.402 --> 03:01.268
and a Honda Civic and a Toyota Camry and a Tesla Y.

03:02.047 --> 03:05.745
But from our conversation last time,

03:05.794 --> 03:09.250
you said you wanted a garage that was useful and

03:09.750 --> 03:12.999
functional instead of being so tight that you couldn't have

03:13.499 --> 03:16.810
both cars and garbage cans in it. And I remember one

03:16.842 --> 03:19.798
of the questions was a concern about the stairs,

03:20.393 --> 03:23.333
if the stairs extended too far into the garage or if there was a landing

03:23.365 --> 03:26.820
that would reduce the usable area of the

03:27.320 --> 03:30.483
garage. And we double-checked the grades and everything,

03:30.821 --> 03:34.436
and all of the garages will be able to have a one-step

03:34.693 --> 03:38.405
maximum. So it's just one step that's inside the garage

03:38.469 --> 03:41.152
and it doesn't affect the usable space.

03:42.167 --> 03:45.076
And the,

03:46.345 --> 03:49.431
the reason why we were doing this, if you'll recall, we had

03:49.496 --> 03:53.621
in our development agreement that there would be 40 townhomes

03:53.686 --> 03:57.252
built that were 3 stories that had a minimum of a 600-square-foot garage.

03:58.026 --> 04:02.059
And we've built 20 of those so far, and they just haven't

04:02.559 --> 04:06.316
been as well received by the market. And so looking

04:06.429 --> 04:09.746
forward at the, the last lots that we have to

04:10.246 --> 04:13.665
build with the larger garage design. We felt like if we were

04:13.681 --> 04:17.166
able to do a 2-story instead of a 3, and with this

04:18.097 --> 04:21.132
updated design that's 24 feet deep,

04:21.871 --> 04:25.259
we wanted to get your input on that to

04:25.759 --> 04:29.643
see what your thoughts were. But we think this could, you know, still offer a

04:30.143 --> 04:33.835
design with a bigger garage area that's

04:34.335 --> 04:37.614
functional, but also would be helpful

04:38.114 --> 04:42.090
to have that interest from the market.

04:42.364 --> 04:47.136
So I'm happy to answer any questions or

04:48.250 --> 04:52.884
hear your thoughts on it. So would these be in lieu of the 3-car

04:52.998 --> 04:56.573
or the 4-car garages, or would you be doing these 2-car— this

04:57.073 --> 05:00.579
new 2-car garage design also on the other units that were already planned as 2-car

05:01.079 --> 05:04.409
garages? So the way the development agreement

05:04.441 --> 05:07.855
was is that there were to be 40 with the larger garage and all

05:07.903 --> 05:10.824
the rest of them would be the standard 2-car design.

05:11.600 --> 05:16.919
And any that we would change would

05:17.291 --> 05:20.552
need to have a plat amendment to the plat, and we want to keep going

05:20.584 --> 05:24.130
forward with the construction of the other ones. So the 20

05:24.630 --> 05:28.834
that have been built are these 4 in Plat 2. These 16

05:29.268 --> 05:32.559
in Plat 3, and then the ones that are left to

05:33.059 --> 05:36.685
build are these 4 fourplexes and 1 fiveplex.

05:37.295 --> 05:40.667
And so what we would propose is we would actually end up

05:41.167 --> 05:43.846
with 41 of the larger garage design instead of 40,

05:44.360 --> 05:47.779
but it would— the 20 that are already done

05:48.052 --> 05:51.873
would count towards the requirement, but we would do it and propose

05:51.970 --> 05:55.419
to do an amendment to the development agreement

05:55.919 --> 06:00.135
to allow 21 of these larger garages to

06:00.589 --> 06:04.205
replace the 20, the 3-story ones, with this

06:04.416 --> 06:05.811
minimum 600-square-foot garage.

06:09.835 --> 06:13.546
So if I'm not mistaken, you're asking if this is palatable so

06:13.611 --> 06:16.580
you could come back and ask for another amendment, right?

06:16.629 --> 06:20.820
That's how we would memorialize the agreement, would be a second

06:21.320 --> 06:25.027
amendment to the development agreement. Does it— is there any feedback?

06:25.123 --> 06:28.793
Any— does this seem to meet the requirement

06:28.809 --> 06:29.533
that we were looking for?

06:32.304 --> 06:36.403
And I, I didn't mention the garage area with this extended

06:36.419 --> 06:40.389
garage is 496 square feet. So, so maybe

06:40.889 --> 06:45.044
to help the council— What's that? What was

06:45.060 --> 06:48.455
the prior square footage of the garage? The prior square

06:48.955 --> 06:51.930
footage was, um, I believe 421.

06:52.430 --> 06:55.850
421. Yeah, of the 2 stories. Yeah. And this

06:56.350 --> 07:00.230
is 496, so. And it's 24 feet

07:00.730 --> 07:03.390
deep. But, but to be clear, I mean, I want to make sure we're understanding

07:03.890 --> 07:06.910
the ask correctly. You're required to build 600 square foot garages

07:07.410 --> 07:11.090
now on those 3 stories, right? Yes, that's correct. And so you're asking to build

07:11.590 --> 07:15.410
a 496 square foot garage in lieu of those 600 square

07:15.910 --> 07:19.714
foot garages because— and then keep it a 2 story. Because it's a little easier

07:19.747 --> 07:23.408
to market. I mean, is that a fair— is that a fair assessment?

07:23.941 --> 07:26.877
When I say the market, it's not like the marketing, like the advertising.

07:27.420 --> 07:31.064
Like we've, we've taken them to market and the— You're just having a hard

07:31.096 --> 07:34.384
time selling them and you want them to sell. And so do we. The buyers

07:34.416 --> 07:38.565
have been tended to gravitate

07:39.065 --> 07:40.374
towards the 2-story more than the 3-story.

07:42.531 --> 07:46.149
So the changes that would come back are 100 square foot less garage,

07:46.454 --> 07:50.420
and these would remain 3-story or 2-story? So these would be a 2-story.

07:50.532 --> 07:54.450
So it's just the 2 changes from 3-story to 2-story

07:54.627 --> 07:58.095
and the garage from 600 to 500. Yeah. Ford. Well,

07:58.400 --> 08:01.499
and so I'm going to ask a question. I mean, let me say this.

08:01.692 --> 08:05.481
Thank you for listening to at least what our concerns were. It's obvious that

08:05.981 --> 08:09.351
you, that you understood the intent of at least what my concerns are, and I

08:09.851 --> 08:13.006
think probably what the council's are in general. You're welcome, and thanks for letting

08:13.022 --> 08:16.237
us know what the current concerns were. But with that said,

08:19.566 --> 08:23.020
I get— I guess for me the question would be, would be simply this: is

08:23.520 --> 08:26.827
there a way to incorporate this into more units that have that

08:27.327 --> 08:30.577
smaller footprint or that smaller garage? I don't know if there is or not.

08:30.673 --> 08:34.389
I mean, that's why I'm asking, because if we're, if we're

08:34.470 --> 08:37.588
willing to consider this, and perhaps we should,

08:37.847 --> 08:41.124
I'm not saying that, that I'm a yes or no

08:41.624 --> 08:44.900
on it, but perhaps we should. But if we're going to do it, I'd like

08:45.400 --> 08:49.060
to know, can we incorporate something beneficial like this into the design of some of

08:49.077 --> 08:52.418
the remaining 2-story units? That's what I'm interested in hearing about.

08:53.539 --> 08:54.720
So, um,

08:56.467 --> 08:59.591
the, the intent, um, had been

08:59.704 --> 09:02.866
to do the, uh, the Platt

09:02.964 --> 09:07.094
Amendment, and that was one One other thing that I guess is

09:07.142 --> 09:10.785
kind of more of a procedural thing on these 4 units.

09:11.074 --> 09:14.509
We did a lot line adjustment, which the state law allows

09:14.605 --> 09:18.297
and is a little faster and easier for us, but our feedback from the staff

09:18.797 --> 09:21.988
was that it's not as easy for them, and so they asked if we would

09:22.488 --> 09:26.242
have our surveyor do a plat amendment rather than a lot line adjustment,

09:26.338 --> 09:30.110
and we're happy to do that to make it easier and better for the staff.

09:30.720 --> 09:34.418
But to answer your question, to get up to the,

09:34.821 --> 09:38.321
the 40, it would actually be 41. We were intending

09:38.370 --> 09:42.064
to do the larger garage on the, the 21 units that

09:42.564 --> 09:45.027
are currently shown with the red 3 for the 3 stories,

09:46.265 --> 09:49.884
and that would kind of get above the 40 with

09:50.384 --> 09:53.935
the larger garage. That was the, the intent of

09:54.435 --> 09:58.085
the original development agreement. Can I restate the question?

09:58.149 --> 10:01.645
I think what Councilmember Wright's asking is that these units up here,

10:02.398 --> 10:05.701
what is the size of the garage on those, and would you increase it

10:05.765 --> 10:09.469
in exchange for decreasing these, make those a little bit more

10:09.969 --> 10:13.461
palatable as well? So the other units would also, you know,

10:13.477 --> 10:16.780
in exchange for making these different, is that right?

10:16.844 --> 10:20.692
That really is the intent of my question, is there a way to

10:21.192 --> 10:25.423
perhaps increase the size of those that aren't required to have the larger size In

10:25.923 --> 10:29.792
exchange for reducing those that are required to be 600 feet to the 496?

10:29.840 --> 10:33.261
That's really the question. I don't know if so architecturally

10:33.309 --> 10:36.634
that's something that can happen, right? So from

10:36.666 --> 10:39.782
an architectural standpoint, we wanted to get feedback before we

10:40.282 --> 10:44.311
had the structural engineers go through and redesign the structure. And this,

10:45.114 --> 10:48.872
this one's already been poured, the footing and foundation walls. It's getting backfilled

10:49.372 --> 10:53.350
right now, and this one is getting dug

10:53.606 --> 10:57.936
and poured in the next week or so. Which ones could

10:58.436 --> 11:02.073
you make an adjustment to if you're able to do so? So I would have

11:02.121 --> 11:03.901
to get with Element Homes on their,

11:05.392 --> 11:08.567
their schedule, but it— in

11:08.663 --> 11:12.030
addition to doing the, the re-engineering of the structural

11:12.530 --> 11:15.590
engineering, we also have to do the plat amendment before we could do it.

11:16.215 --> 11:19.682
So our thought had been to do these 5 Last,

11:19.827 --> 11:23.910
so there would be time for the plat amendment to happen. But this

11:24.103 --> 11:27.430
one we were hesitant to because we've already installed the trail. But if we had

11:27.930 --> 11:32.180
to tear out the trail and redo it, we could. These two,

11:33.603 --> 11:36.642
it would really depend on the timing of the plat amendment. So I,

11:37.176 --> 11:40.270
I don't want to dodge the question, but I don't have enough information to answer.

11:40.335 --> 11:43.382
But we, we could get with Element Homes and see what their schedule is.

11:43.577 --> 11:47.298
Get with kind of the planning department and see how long they think the—

11:47.603 --> 11:50.398
and our surveyor— and see how long they think the plat amendment could take.

11:50.446 --> 11:52.711
So I've kind of hogged the time, and I'm going to just leave this.

11:52.840 --> 11:56.486
I'll give you the, my last little bit, and then, and then step back and,

11:56.598 --> 11:59.859
and let the other council members give you some thoughts. I'd be willing

11:59.891 --> 12:03.553
to entertain what you're asking if there is some additional

12:03.585 --> 12:06.766
benefit in creating better off-street parking for these

12:07.199 --> 12:10.652
residents that are over and above those 40 that you're already obligated to do

12:11.152 --> 12:14.677
that on. If all you're going to do is change a 600-square-foot garage

12:15.177 --> 12:18.574
for a 500-square-foot and go from a 3-story to 2-story, I don't care. To me,

12:18.590 --> 12:21.043
that's not, that's not a huge upside.

12:22.117 --> 12:25.854
I mean, it's actually a downside from my perspective in that we're losing,

12:26.190 --> 12:29.991
we're reducing space in those garages. But if you were able to

12:30.491 --> 12:34.689
say, look, we want to do these 496-square-foot garages and

12:35.189 --> 12:39.038
we're willing to incorporate some better garage design to To

12:39.538 --> 12:43.392
incorporate the feedback that we heard council give to ensure that the

12:43.456 --> 12:47.216
people who either purchase these or rent them will have access

12:47.312 --> 12:51.088
to a useful garage. That's that's really what we're talking about,

12:51.714 --> 12:55.474
and they'll be able to store their their their their garbage cans

12:55.490 --> 12:58.800
inside and get two vehicles. I like the vehicles that you used.

12:59.202 --> 13:02.222
I think those are very appropriate. I think

13:02.238 --> 13:05.867
that that many of our residents. Are going to have 2

13:06.367 --> 13:09.912
vehicles in a similar setup. And it makes sense. It gets them inside.

13:09.960 --> 13:13.170
It gives them some protection. And it also keeps them off the street.

13:13.667 --> 13:17.166
So for me, at least, I'd be willing to entertain that if—

13:17.666 --> 13:21.291
I'd be willing to entertain going from the 600

13:21.791 --> 13:26.058
to 496, let you go from 3-story to 2-story if there's

13:26.122 --> 13:29.861
some additional benefit in those units that aren't required to have the larger

13:30.343 --> 13:33.759
That's really what I'm looking for, is, is there a chance to do that?

13:34.369 --> 13:38.283
Okay. Mayor, can you go back to

13:38.783 --> 13:42.518
the one that shows like the overall— I think it

13:43.018 --> 13:45.228
really comes down to parking,

13:46.047 --> 13:49.944
and I'm grateful that one, you're listening to the market,

13:50.634 --> 13:54.356
and two, that you've made an attempt to change, because certainly we've

13:54.856 --> 13:58.592
had developers come in here 3, 4 times And every

13:59.092 --> 14:02.527
single time it's the same stupid thing. And it's like, you guys didn't listen to

14:03.027 --> 14:06.205
us last time, but you like listened. So I appreciate the effort to make a

14:06.705 --> 14:10.896
change. I guess one of the suggestions that I would give, and I'm going

14:11.396 --> 14:13.996
to kind of look to my fellow council members to see if this would be

14:14.496 --> 14:18.108
enough, what if they added some additional overflow parking spots

14:18.573 --> 14:22.380
in the neighborhood where those homes have the smaller

14:22.509 --> 14:26.188
garages? What if, even if it came down to

14:26.688 --> 14:29.787
taking one lot out to add way more overflow parking?

14:30.301 --> 14:33.549
Because I think that ultimately would be cheaper

14:33.742 --> 14:38.179
than a full redesign of ones that are already the

14:38.679 --> 14:42.567
2-story ones. So I'm just wondering if that would make

14:43.067 --> 14:44.664
a big enough difference for my fellow council members.

14:47.350 --> 14:50.794
I've got some questions for you. What's the difference in the width of the garage

14:50.925 --> 14:54.411
from your 3, your 3-story model?

14:55.311 --> 14:59.264
So the, the width is the same. It's 20.08

14:59.764 --> 15:03.490
feet. And the reason for that is the lots are already platted

15:03.827 --> 15:07.234
and we can't make— and all of the utilities are stubbed to them.

15:07.298 --> 15:10.865
That leads to my next question. And so basically we've given up the storage space

15:10.897 --> 15:14.368
behind that was there in the 3-story. We've gone with a

15:14.868 --> 15:18.779
longer garage per se that will enable trucks garbage

15:18.844 --> 15:22.588
cans and even some SUVs to be in there, but we haven't really gained

15:23.088 --> 15:24.394
any ground on the width of the garage.

15:26.169 --> 15:29.876
That's correct. One thing we have gained on though is the original— when

15:29.892 --> 15:33.495
we did the development agreement, the 2 stories did not have a

15:33.995 --> 15:38.330
basement, and now the, the design with the 2 stories has a 520

15:38.830 --> 15:44.439
square foot unfinished basement. So the 21 Townhomes

15:44.456 --> 15:48.047
that we're proposing to have this 2-story design with the larger garage,

15:49.714 --> 15:54.136
it'd be 520 times 21, about 11,000

15:54.168 --> 15:57.845
square feet of— I actually did the calculation,

15:57.894 --> 16:02.701
let me look— 11,000 square feet of unfinished

16:02.733 --> 16:05.982
basement storage space that the 3 stories would not have because they're

16:06.482 --> 16:10.325
slab on grade. So my, my concern is width.

16:11.048 --> 16:13.768
I think you should probably remember that from our last conversation.

16:14.992 --> 16:16.875
They need to be able to get out of the car once it's in there,

16:16.940 --> 16:20.417
especially when it's snowing, raining, or whatever. And if they

16:20.450 --> 16:23.165
pull in at the same time, it really is gonna be the finagling that we

16:23.456 --> 16:26.769
tend to see where everybody gets out and somebody backs the car in to get

16:27.269 --> 16:30.519
out. Do you feel like that there's any flexibility to

16:31.019 --> 16:34.197
move that to a 21.5-foot width inside?

16:35.244 --> 16:39.048
Where if it was a paper lot and it was just

16:39.291 --> 16:42.712
kind of a concept, might be possible, but where the roads are

16:42.776 --> 16:46.032
paved and all the utilities are in and all of the— You'd end up losing

16:46.048 --> 16:50.346
the use of those for stuff. The lots, it would be— I'm

16:50.394 --> 16:54.002
less concerned about the parking. I mean, it's a townhouse community. I mean, there's only

16:54.502 --> 16:57.819
so many places you can put parking. I think that you've, you've shown,

16:57.916 --> 17:01.572
at least on this design, that there's space to be

17:02.072 --> 17:05.309
able to park on the driveway. You can fit inside the garage. I think

17:05.809 --> 17:09.575
that's the intent, at least from My perspective, the width is a

17:09.944 --> 17:13.248
bit of a concern. I don't think we gained any ground on that, and I

17:13.748 --> 17:17.740
don't know that I'm one way or the other not

17:18.240 --> 17:21.301
having the space behind the garage. I think they'll likely use that

17:21.493 --> 17:25.503
for a storage space more than they would use the basement for that. The basement

17:26.003 --> 17:29.786
just ends up being a catch-all, but that's a decision you

17:30.286 --> 17:32.641
guys make. I don't care about that stuff. That's really not us. That's that's going

17:33.141 --> 17:36.530
to be market driven and whether people agree. with that. I think for me,

17:36.835 --> 17:40.054
what we were looking for as far as the parking, I think you've accomplished that

17:40.296 --> 17:44.141
in my perspective. Um, I, I do like

17:44.641 --> 17:47.990
that the floor plan is, uh, a little more palatable

17:48.087 --> 17:50.415
to the people who want to buy it and don't have smaller floors.

17:52.538 --> 17:55.501
So for me, I don't, I don't really have any problems with this.

17:56.612 --> 17:59.833
Great, thank you. I'm, I'm along the lines of what Council

18:00.333 --> 18:03.515
Member Wright's asking, is that if we're giving up space And taking a

18:03.580 --> 18:07.300
3-car and going to a 2-car, if you can improve the 2 cars

18:07.800 --> 18:11.021
in the other areas that were smaller and you can make those 2 cars larger,

18:12.236 --> 18:15.698
then I would be open to looking at that for sure. So if that's

18:16.198 --> 18:18.989
something you could bring back, I would be open to looking at that. Okay,

18:19.005 --> 18:25.878
let's go back to the other page. So you only mentioned a couple. So what

18:26.411 --> 18:29.494
they've both said, you mentioned that these ones aren't done, but also these ones.

18:29.670 --> 18:33.181
So we're looking at this row and this row that still possibly could

18:33.681 --> 18:36.458
be adjusted. That's what you're asking? Yeah.

18:36.652 --> 18:39.403
And I, again, I don't wanna commit to it. I need to find out.

18:39.823 --> 18:42.800
Well, yeah, I was just out there, I think. Could you state your name for

18:43.300 --> 18:47.280
the record? Oh, sorry. Taylor Keyes with Element Homes

18:47.780 --> 18:51.076
on the sales part of it. I was out there. I believe they've got

18:52.440 --> 18:55.366
this blue. You need to point at it. Here's one of the blue that you're

18:55.414 --> 18:59.247
using. This one's getting backfilled. That one's getting backfilled, and I think they've

18:59.747 --> 19:03.589
dug here. And I guess what I— we'll have to look

19:04.089 --> 19:06.987
at and see what's been submitted, right? But if I'm hearing what you're saying is

19:07.003 --> 19:09.470
go back and say, well, what buildings could be on the table?

19:10.127 --> 19:12.980
So whether that's a function of we just have to say, okay, here's how many

19:13.480 --> 19:16.217
have been pulled, here's what's on the schedule, and then we come back and say,

19:16.233 --> 19:19.582
well, we could do this building or not do this building and kind of see

19:20.082 --> 19:23.090
what that— if that fits what you're looking for. Ultimately, I think the goal here

19:23.463 --> 19:27.790
is to gain more than just the additional 20— Well,

19:28.001 --> 19:31.494
this would be 21 instead of 20. Yeah, and 20, right.

19:31.558 --> 19:34.818
So I think I'm hearing what you're saying is you

19:34.850 --> 19:38.498
would like it to be a greater benefit if we're gonna do this. Well,

19:38.514 --> 19:41.843
it might not be the exact same

19:41.908 --> 19:45.448
as these other units you're talking about here, but at least bigger than what's already

19:45.948 --> 19:49.295
designed as a trade-off. Sure. 'Cause I don't know what the design is

19:49.327 --> 19:52.570
on those. I don't remember. Well, so we had the— so around the perimeter there,

19:52.939 --> 19:56.681
it's the typical 2-story with the garage that was originally approved,

19:56.697 --> 20:00.085
the smaller garage, right? And then we have this 2-story with

20:00.585 --> 20:04.163
the bigger garage. And so the question would just be what units could

20:04.663 --> 20:07.936
we— So it would be easier to do the ones around

20:08.436 --> 20:11.453
the outside just because there's— There's more space. Space towards the boundary.

20:11.645 --> 20:15.187
On these ones, it would be taking up, know, the grass open space

20:15.687 --> 20:18.494
or going into the trail. But so that's something— it sounds like if you can

20:18.994 --> 20:22.286
make that happen, you'll probably get a yes. I, I think we can

20:22.786 --> 20:25.663
take that feedback and get back with the team, and then we can bring it.

20:25.969 --> 20:29.569
Zach, you're going on or something?

20:29.858 --> 20:32.826
Okay. So yeah, I'm gonna— I, I would agree with Brett and Craig.

20:33.577 --> 20:36.660
If we can get a little more value there, come back with that, I think

20:37.160 --> 20:40.373
that'd be fine. Okay, so I'm going to sum up what I've heard,

20:40.389 --> 20:45.233
and if I missed something, let me know. These units on this outside potentially

20:45.427 --> 20:46.753
adding a little bit of garage space.

20:48.950 --> 20:51.878
These ones weren't on the table anyway, so it's just these ones out here.

20:53.382 --> 20:55.972
Maybe some additional parking.

20:56.261 --> 20:59.929
These ones are all built on the south boundary. No, yeah, I was just pointing

20:59.945 --> 21:03.603
out the parking here. So these ones get increased a little bit

21:03.798 --> 21:07.827
in exchange for these ones getting decreased. And then a potential additional

21:07.859 --> 21:11.825
parking similar to this guest parking somewhere. So I

21:11.873 --> 21:15.791
was zooming in on the tabulations when the civil engineer designed

21:16.291 --> 21:19.869
it. The required parking was 378 and the provided

21:19.901 --> 21:24.558
parking was 386, and we had them add parking basically

21:25.058 --> 21:28.235
everywhere that they could. You know, there's a lot of places where it's just 2

21:28.735 --> 21:32.667
parking spots wide because it's in between the So,

21:32.812 --> 21:35.688
and I heard Councilmember Wood say that he was less concerned about that. So is

21:35.961 --> 21:39.319
parking— is more of just a maybe? There's just not a place to

21:39.819 --> 21:42.694
put it. Like, if they've already got the footprints planned, I mean,

21:42.710 --> 21:46.132
you're going to have to start giving up units. I don't think that's

21:46.164 --> 21:49.619
a feasible solution. So the parking's— we're okay with the parking, then it's

21:50.119 --> 21:53.459
just getting some added benefit in these outside units. And I think

21:53.959 --> 21:57.171
then, then we'd consider the— is that good? Anything else?

21:57.958 --> 22:01.541
Councilmember Clark, is that kind of— is that good feedback? Do you have

22:01.589 --> 22:04.802
any questions? Yes, it is. And, and there's a handful of places that

22:05.302 --> 22:07.822
don't have the parking space, but I believe that's where we put the transformers.

22:07.951 --> 22:11.132
So, um, yeah, we'll go back and, and get with

22:11.632 --> 22:15.292
Element. I know this one, the building permits are already submitted and,

22:15.485 --> 22:18.955
and it's going to be dug soon, but these ones around the outside,

22:19.566 --> 22:22.859
we'll get with both the structural engineer and the surveyor and

22:23.359 --> 22:27.138
the planning department and see You know, on both the replatting and

22:27.638 --> 22:31.212
reengineering, and see how quick that that could potentially happen. And thank you

22:31.228 --> 22:35.187
for working with our staff on the replat versus the lot line adjustment.

22:35.268 --> 22:37.502
I mean, if they're asking you to do that, I'm grateful that you're willing to

22:38.002 --> 22:40.766
entertain that. Yeah, and the reason we did the lot line adjustment on this one

22:40.799 --> 22:44.455
just is our surveyor said it's faster. But looking at

22:44.988 --> 22:48.359
how much work it takes to get it updated

22:48.391 --> 22:52.532
with the county, I can see why. The staff prefers the replat, so we're

22:52.564 --> 22:55.808
happy to do that. Okay, thank you for— Do you have any other questions?

22:55.856 --> 22:58.730
Okay, I think we're good. Thank you very much. Appreciate you coming in. Thank you.

22:59.725 --> 23:03.049
Getting through that. All right, so next item,

23:03.161 --> 23:06.276
1B, is a discussion about Healthy Utah Communities.

23:06.854 --> 23:10.001
This is something that the council, an initiative we've— we talk about

23:10.501 --> 23:13.196
every year when we're in St. George, and I believe in Salt Lake. Um,

23:14.256 --> 23:18.030
I know the deadline's coming up, so Steve is gonna lead

23:18.530 --> 23:21.201
this, tell us what we gotta do,

23:21.913 --> 23:25.362
get some direction. I know the Community Services

23:25.862 --> 23:29.109
Board also worked on this, I believe. And if there are

23:29.609 --> 23:30.135
any other questions, go ahead.

23:33.679 --> 23:37.623
Thank you, Mayor. We'll see if it comes up here. It's not coming up.

23:37.949 --> 23:38.845
Okay.

23:45.294 --> 23:48.203
I may have to go hang out in Dave's spot over there. We've got help

23:48.703 --> 23:52.324
coming from the back row. Should be

23:52.824 --> 23:55.747
as easy as plugging in and going. I'll give an intro then.

23:57.959 --> 24:01.090
Yeah, we always go to these conferences in the fall and the spring, and then

24:01.122 --> 24:04.301
we hear about all these communities that are doing great things related to

24:04.350 --> 24:07.537
health and food and mental health.

24:07.926 --> 24:11.732
And active living. And then we're always like, why aren't we

24:12.232 --> 24:16.212
doing this? Let's get going. And so Natalie Winterton actually

24:16.228 --> 24:20.227
did some work on this last year to get us going. And the

24:20.727 --> 24:24.305
city council last year approved a resolution to

24:24.386 --> 24:28.031
authorize the mayor to sign a letter and start the application process.

24:28.095 --> 24:32.142
And then we were lacking in a couple of areas that we couldn't fulfill

24:32.190 --> 24:35.780
back then. But now that we have our community services board in place, that can

24:35.878 --> 24:39.227
act as our health coalition.

24:40.370 --> 24:42.114
We hope that that will work for it.

24:45.132 --> 24:48.610
The Get Healthy Utah organization, the 501 Get Healthy

24:48.707 --> 24:51.993
Utah, put together this

24:52.493 --> 24:55.993
community healthy— Healthy

24:56.139 --> 24:59.508
Utah Community Designation specifically for towns and cities.

25:00.997 --> 25:04.249
And our size requires that we

25:04.557 --> 25:08.084
implement 3, that we have already implemented

25:08.196 --> 25:11.715
3 action items, 3 strategies within

25:12.149 --> 25:15.507
3 different categories. And the first is active

25:15.523 --> 25:17.339
living, and then there's,

25:20.464 --> 25:24.616
it's related to food services, access to healthy

25:24.649 --> 25:28.442
food, and then— Housing. The last is mental health. And so

25:29.963 --> 25:33.700
at the request of the council, I started working with our Community Services

25:34.200 --> 25:38.213
Board, with Jesse and Charlotte and the board, and we

25:38.713 --> 25:42.134
talked in a couple of different meetings. We had other priorities pop up, so we've

25:42.634 --> 25:44.930
been talking about it for probably a few meetings, but this last meeting we really

25:45.430 --> 25:49.493
dove in and chose some strategies that

25:49.993 --> 25:53.846
we've already implemented, and then— What are those strategies? Also recommended

25:53.862 --> 25:59.064
some 3 additional strategies that we need to propose

25:59.883 --> 26:03.351
to implement over the next 3 years. I can

26:03.851 --> 26:06.563
just plug in over there if we need to. Yeah, it's probably my computer.

26:07.606 --> 26:08.810
Okay, hang tight for a moment.

26:23.451 --> 26:26.772
Okay, we'll try this one, Lacey, if that— and if it's my computer,

26:26.821 --> 26:32.384
it won't pop up, so we will— yeah,

26:32.464 --> 26:33.273
all right, great.

26:39.241 --> 26:41.715
Okay, so passed the resolution last year,

26:42.151 --> 26:45.529
requires 9 implemented strategies and 3 strategies within each of those categories.

26:49.455 --> 26:52.170
And so this just gives you an idea. They have a list of, hey,

26:52.186 --> 26:55.928
here are some recommended strategies within the active living category.

26:56.281 --> 26:59.542
There's a bunch of different things. We can go back to that if, if you

26:59.574 --> 27:03.091
want. Same with access to healthy food and

27:03.187 --> 27:06.608
mental health. And so the strategies

27:06.721 --> 27:10.174
that the board chose to recommend to have

27:10.674 --> 27:14.634
shown that we implemented already or are currently maintaining.

27:15.933 --> 27:19.123
First was to organize a free or low-cost fitness event for your community,

27:19.636 --> 27:23.852
and we do the annual Turkey Trot every year. It's on Thanksgiving

27:24.125 --> 27:27.379
morning, brings out thousands of people. They love

27:27.395 --> 27:31.660
it. That's one of our event staff's favorite

27:32.160 --> 27:35.972
events. And then second was to create an active transportation

27:36.472 --> 27:39.916
plan or update an existing one. So we do have an existing bike and pedestrian

27:40.416 --> 27:43.797
master plan that needs some more updating soon.

27:44.727 --> 27:47.982
But you, council, also approved the off-street trails

27:48.014 --> 27:51.670
master plan just last meeting. And then third,

27:51.686 --> 27:55.776
improve physical activity among the youth by promoting a physical activity resource

27:55.792 --> 27:59.800
program or best practice in schools. The example they gave on

28:00.300 --> 28:03.569
their website is, for example, safe routes to schools. And so we talked about

28:04.069 --> 28:07.975
that. And the schools all create a plan

28:08.315 --> 28:12.554
and then the city helps as much as we can to implement that.

28:13.318 --> 28:16.554
And it's not just having sidewalks and

28:17.054 --> 28:20.630
trails to the school, but it also includes from the school side other

28:21.808 --> 28:25.905
trainings and practices,

28:26.421 --> 28:29.889
activities to help encourage kids to bike and walk to school.

28:32.244 --> 28:35.648
So that's the active living. There were other options

28:35.680 --> 28:38.779
there as well, but those are the 3 that the board recommended.

28:39.149 --> 28:42.954
Second is access to healthy food. So the first was host a community

28:42.986 --> 28:46.888
program to promote healthy eating. And our library

28:47.000 --> 28:51.126
has had a seed exchange that's for vegetables and other seeds that they've

28:51.626 --> 28:54.578
had for quite a while. And that's pretty popular in there.

28:55.590 --> 28:58.930
And then improve access to emergency food by establishing a food pantry

28:59.042 --> 29:02.317
or program or improving an existing one. I think you're all

29:02.817 --> 29:06.073
aware of the food pantry that we have in the library, the emergency food pantry

29:06.185 --> 29:09.588
that's, that's right there in the entryway, and that's been well

29:09.700 --> 29:13.311
utilized by our community. And then establish a farmers market

29:13.811 --> 29:17.387
that accepts SNAP benefits. I've been told that that's what our farmers

29:17.419 --> 29:20.629
market does, that we partner with that group and, and they're there every

29:21.129 --> 29:24.321
Saturday morning during the summer at Quarry Ride Park. And then we've also

29:24.821 --> 29:28.556
included the farmers market in some of our major events recently as well.

29:29.985 --> 29:33.668
And then last is mental health. First one was

29:34.493 --> 29:38.070
we decided to use the other category, and it's to promote a program

29:38.570 --> 29:42.389
to foster encouragement, connection, and hope across the community. And so the council

29:43.163 --> 29:46.953
passed the resolution to become

29:47.453 --> 29:50.308
the first Believer City with the Show Me a Sign organization.

29:51.383 --> 29:53.800
And then we have that event as well, the Motivational Mile.

29:54.860 --> 29:58.667
Second is host a recurring community event that promotes social connection among

29:58.715 --> 30:02.731
a vulnerable demographic. And so one of our vulnerable demographics

30:03.231 --> 30:06.826
is our senior citizens, and the council's been great to support

30:06.874 --> 30:10.231
them with funding and with a location that we expanded

30:10.729 --> 30:14.279
fairly recently. And then they have all of their events

30:14.779 --> 30:18.310
that they participate in. And then third is implement a strategy to

30:18.358 --> 30:22.129
promote safe use of guns. And we do provide free gun locks at our

30:22.145 --> 30:25.848
library and also can promote that

30:26.634 --> 30:30.112
on social media and to our residents. So those are, those are the 9

30:30.737 --> 30:34.376
strategies that were recommended that we include. And,

30:34.713 --> 30:38.271
and then the next section is to, is to show the data

30:38.415 --> 30:41.734
and look at, look at the data that's out there and be able to determine,

30:41.862 --> 30:45.084
okay, what are our greatest needs as a community so that we can implement 3

30:45.584 --> 30:48.967
new strategies over the next 3 years. We didn't have

30:48.999 --> 30:52.179
time or take the time to go through this in detail with the Community Services

30:52.291 --> 30:55.038
Board. They did recommend some strategies, which I'll show you,

30:56.066 --> 30:59.792
but I wanted to show you some of the research from these, these 3

30:59.824 --> 31:01.382
locations that are recommended.

31:02.811 --> 31:05.268
Public Health Indicator-Based Information System,

31:06.007 --> 31:09.798
which is a— which is on the Department of Health and Human Services website.

31:11.066 --> 31:14.455
The Utah Healthy Places Index, which is more of a map system, and you

31:14.955 --> 31:18.486
go in and it shows some It breaks up basically by

31:18.986 --> 31:22.714
tax areas, breaks up the city and

31:23.214 --> 31:26.566
the whole state. And then Utah State University Well-Being

31:26.663 --> 31:29.499
Survey, which they just barely released the results from that.

31:30.910 --> 31:34.576
So all I did here was include some of the areas that we're

31:35.322 --> 31:40.629
either doing poorly in our community or close

31:41.129 --> 31:44.524
to. Or that maybe I was concerned about.

31:45.873 --> 31:50.274
And so one is the fair— is fair or poor general health. And it's

31:50.774 --> 31:54.353
a percentage of adults aged 18 and older who reported fair or

31:54.369 --> 31:57.678
poor general health. And in our community, that was 16.6%.

31:57.983 --> 32:01.645
That's higher than the Utah average, lower than the US average.

32:02.641 --> 32:05.885
That was a little concerning. 7 or more days of poor mental

32:05.917 --> 32:07.829
health in the past 30 days.

32:10.188 --> 32:12.972
21% of our residents are adults.

32:15.295 --> 32:19.310
That's under the state average and the national average, but still concerning. And then

32:19.342 --> 32:22.710
vegetables consumed 3 or more times per day, just showing do you have a good

32:22.726 --> 32:25.998
healthy mix of food available in our— where there's

32:26.498 --> 32:29.968
a red exclamation point, that means we're lower or worse

32:30.032 --> 32:31.475
than the Utah average.

32:35.176 --> 32:38.211
Another was high blood pressure or high blood cholesterol.

32:39.389 --> 32:41.858
Ours was quite a bit higher than the Utah average.

32:43.268 --> 32:46.404
And hypertension, doctor-diagnosed hypertension

32:46.904 --> 32:47.981
as well was higher.

32:50.732 --> 32:54.144
The recommended amount of aerobic physical activity, and so

32:54.678 --> 32:58.268
recommended amount for adults it looks like is 150

32:58.768 --> 33:02.522
minutes per week of moderate-intensity or 75 minutes of vigorous intensity.

33:04.421 --> 33:07.745
And it says we're about in line

33:08.245 --> 33:11.945
with Utah, but we're about 9% lower than the Utah

33:12.042 --> 33:15.549
average. Adult obesity and

33:15.678 --> 33:19.363
overweight or obese adults, ours was

33:20.138 --> 33:23.732
slightly higher than Utah in

33:23.748 --> 33:24.786
both of those categories.

33:26.894 --> 33:30.505
And then how well are our female

33:30.619 --> 33:34.441
adults looking ahead and planning

33:34.941 --> 33:38.593
for mammograms and Pap tests and others? And our rates

33:39.093 --> 33:40.321
were quite a bit lower than Utah.

33:43.633 --> 33:47.514
And almost done with these, but the suicide rate is lower

33:48.367 --> 33:52.424
than the Utah average, but obviously it's one of those zero fatalities kind

33:52.924 --> 33:56.229
of number that you don't want. to be high at all, if possible. So that's

33:56.278 --> 33:59.420
concerning. And then depression prevalence as well.

34:02.030 --> 34:05.943
Percentage of adults who have been diagnosed with a

34:06.007 --> 34:09.598
depressive disorder, major depression, minor depression.

34:10.274 --> 34:14.376
Ours is lower than Utah average, but still 23%

34:14.876 --> 34:18.860
of our adult population. And then on the wellbeing survey,

34:19.840 --> 34:23.186
For Utah State, the average rating

34:23.364 --> 34:27.631
of personal and community well-being. So they rate, they rank each

34:27.680 --> 34:30.901
of those. And the last time they did this survey,

34:30.966 --> 34:34.472
they're comparing it to the 2020 results. And so our numbers have

34:34.520 --> 34:36.798
gone down on both of those, it looks like.

34:39.999 --> 34:43.909
And well-being domain importance. How important are the following

34:43.942 --> 34:47.712
categories to your overall personal well-being? And they have quite a few categories

34:48.212 --> 34:51.497
there on the left. Mental health is, and safety and

34:51.997 --> 34:55.355
security are the most important to our residents, 96%.

34:56.097 --> 34:59.550
Living standards, family life close behind, physical health,

34:59.682 --> 35:02.836
environmental quality, leisure time, and then it goes down from there.

35:04.829 --> 35:08.210
So just showing those are really important, even if we're doing fairly

35:08.242 --> 35:12.040
well in some of those categories, they're very important. So we should keep

35:12.540 --> 35:16.496
putting efforts towards improving those areas. How connected

35:16.996 --> 35:20.549
do you feel to Eagle Mountain as a community? Looks like most of

35:20.582 --> 35:24.064
the respondents at least felt average

35:24.564 --> 35:29.398
or below connected to the community or

35:29.430 --> 35:33.843
as a community. And then inclusion for individuals with disabilities.

35:33.939 --> 35:37.172
How well does Eagle Mountain include individuals with disabilities in social,

35:37.205 --> 35:41.056
recreational, and cultural activities? 43% probably didn't

35:41.556 --> 35:43.078
have anyone or know anyone that was disabled.

35:45.116 --> 35:48.498
The rest looks like somewhat, quite well, somewhere in between.

35:49.126 --> 35:52.041
There's always more we can do, of course, with that.

35:53.620 --> 35:56.439
So the board,

35:56.874 --> 36:01.465
again, they didn't go through all of that data, but they recommended 3

36:01.481 --> 36:05.082
strategies, that we destigmatize mental illness through a community

36:05.098 --> 36:08.707
campaign or event. And there were several events talked

36:09.207 --> 36:12.560
about. The high school is involved

36:13.060 --> 36:16.018
in a suicide walk, suicide prevention walk.

36:18.354 --> 36:22.226
And then add wayfinding signage for trails that provide both time and distance for

36:22.242 --> 36:26.259
routes, or maybe conduct a messaging campaign to promote

36:26.759 --> 36:31.103
physical activity in the community. And third

36:31.668 --> 36:34.752
is another category, which is to implement adaptive sports and

36:35.252 --> 36:38.550
recreation programs. Or host a recurring community event that

36:39.050 --> 36:43.493
promotes social connection among a vulnerable demographic, which could be our disabled

36:43.766 --> 36:48.228
population. So I know our Jason Hall and our recreation

36:48.245 --> 36:52.338
group have been very interested in implementing some sort of adaptive program

36:52.838 --> 36:55.580
that's that's more robust than what we do currently.

36:56.286 --> 36:59.914
And currently, we just kind of combine with

36:59.962 --> 37:03.959
the rest of the cities in the in Utah County and

37:06.319 --> 37:09.837
Participants that are disabled, their families have to drive them all over the

37:10.337 --> 37:13.804
place to participate. So one day they'll be in Lehi,

37:13.868 --> 37:16.887
and one day they'll be in Eagle Mountain, and another day they'll be down in

37:18.349 --> 37:22.043
Orem or somewhere. And so it'd be nice if they could stay here and continue

37:22.543 --> 37:26.395
to participate. Steve, there's actually a woman that has been at the farmers

37:26.895 --> 37:29.848
market that is promoting the Special Olympics in Eagle Mountain. She's trying to bring a

37:30.348 --> 37:32.642
chapter here. And so I just want to put that on the radar of the

37:33.142 --> 37:36.464
Community Services Board. I would love to see us find a way to help them

37:37.058 --> 37:40.446
as a vulnerable demographic and see if we can incorporate them into some of our

37:40.946 --> 37:44.219
city events. Yeah, that's a great idea. Yeah, she stopped by our booth at the,

37:44.524 --> 37:48.169
at the farmers market. We all talked to her. That's a great idea. Um,

37:49.196 --> 37:53.500
these were other options that were discussed or thrown out there as

37:53.532 --> 37:57.333
possibilities: establish a community garden, We do

37:57.833 --> 38:01.506
have a couple board members looking into that. Develop a new walking/biking trail

38:01.894 --> 38:05.129
or add to an existing one. Hey Steve, just, I wanna jump back to that

38:05.178 --> 38:08.065
community garden. Is that possible to do that in multiple locations?

38:09.048 --> 38:11.562
Absolutely. I mean, with the size that we are, it'd be great to have that

38:12.062 --> 38:15.531
in a bunch of locations. Could I, just for context,

38:15.660 --> 38:18.913
I know he's wiggling over there about this, but Brad, we already

38:19.413 --> 38:23.122
have some places, right, that you've, that we've identified. Water run there and they kind

38:23.138 --> 38:26.762
