little bit of a higher-level view of some of the bigger projects.

01:13:21.478 --> 01:13:24.994
And we'll, uh, Mayor, we talked about keeping people's mental health

01:13:25.091 --> 01:13:28.566
good. Are you sure this is a good idea? I don't know.

01:13:31.066 --> 01:13:33.322
It's, it's never good when you're looking at me. I don't, I don't know.

01:13:33.903 --> 01:13:37.319
I have that effect on people. But Mayor and Council, thank you

01:13:37.367 --> 01:13:39.447
so much for the opportunity to visit with you a little bit.

01:13:40.834 --> 01:13:44.341
And, uh, it's important to note, especially for the public,

01:13:44.357 --> 01:13:47.573
that There's really 3

01:13:48.073 --> 01:13:51.653
main things that the city gets involved in. It gets involved

01:13:52.153 --> 01:13:55.813
in day-to-day operations, and that's not included on this

01:13:55.925 --> 01:13:58.800
list. So there's a lot of things that we do on a day-to-day basis to

01:13:59.300 --> 01:14:03.443
make sure that people have high-quality water and that that water is processed properly afterwards

01:14:03.475 --> 01:14:06.816
and that they have parks. And, and so there's work that we do day in

01:14:06.912 --> 01:14:10.125
and day out to make the high quality of life that we have for the

01:14:10.625 --> 01:14:13.080
residents. Possible here in Eagle Mountain City.

01:14:13.835 --> 01:14:17.416
Then of course there's emergency response, and that happens all the time as

01:14:17.448 --> 01:14:19.809
well. I know you get texts from me in the middle of the night,

01:14:20.163 --> 01:14:23.793
hey, this lift station went down, or this water tank

01:14:24.293 --> 01:14:27.968
is having an issue, or this well's having a problem, or whatnot. So that's not

01:14:28.468 --> 01:14:31.935
part of this either. Really what we're talking about today is our

01:14:31.984 --> 01:14:35.421
projects that we have, and I want to give maybe a little

01:14:35.921 --> 01:14:39.226
bit of definition to that. We have what we would call capital projects,

01:14:39.612 --> 01:14:42.422
and that's something that would be capitalized at the end, that there would be a

01:14:42.922 --> 01:14:46.291
technical asset at the end, like a water tank. You've got a tank,

01:14:46.340 --> 01:14:50.016
here is a project. It starts off with trying

01:14:50.049 --> 01:14:53.870
to figure out the funding, trying to come up with the initial design,

01:14:54.464 --> 01:14:58.205
do land acquisitions, going out to bid. We have all sorts of

01:14:58.237 --> 01:15:01.690
processes that we are required to follow, set by code,

01:15:01.706 --> 01:15:04.909
set by you, the council. council so that we can make sure that we're

01:15:05.231 --> 01:15:08.355
doing things properly and we're doing it in accordance with not only the

01:15:08.435 --> 01:15:11.511
laws and ordinances of the City of Eagle Mountain but with the state of Utah

01:15:11.577 --> 01:15:15.763
and federally as well. We also have projects

01:15:16.263 --> 01:15:19.612
that are not necessarily capital projects but just projects that we would

01:15:19.628 --> 01:15:23.333
kick off and take on that would still maybe have an asset at the end.

01:15:24.206 --> 01:15:27.426
For example, we're looking at the observatory,

01:15:27.782 --> 01:15:31.871
and so That's one where we're getting donations

01:15:31.903 --> 01:15:35.758
coming in, and there's a lot of work in the background where we're getting grants

01:15:36.258 --> 01:15:39.629
and donations and whatnot come together. There's a lot of work up front, but it's

01:15:39.677 --> 01:15:43.548
not something that you're funding as a true full-on capital project.

01:15:44.335 --> 01:15:47.676
There's something that I would call an initiative. So maybe we have

01:15:48.029 --> 01:15:51.402
a really good idea come forward. Maybe we want to look into a

01:15:51.902 --> 01:15:55.432
justice court. Maybe we want to look into solid

01:15:55.932 --> 01:15:59.738
waste and large— getting rid of solid waste, large items and whatnot, and new

01:15:59.803 --> 01:16:03.284
programs that we can offer to enhance the quality of life of our residents.

01:16:04.392 --> 01:16:08.118
Those often take a lot of steps as well with many departments involved.

01:16:09.510 --> 01:16:12.682
And so it's sort of like a project, but it's really an initiative. There's not

01:16:13.182 --> 01:16:16.146
like an asset at the end. It's really a new program or a new offering

01:16:16.162 --> 01:16:20.076
that might come forward. And then sometimes we just have some simply hard tasks

01:16:20.576 --> 01:16:23.675
that are really time-intensive Maybe we're trying to do a certain land acquisition to support

01:16:24.175 --> 01:16:27.833
the school district or something of that nature. So consequently,

01:16:28.026 --> 01:16:31.558
I've been pulling information from various lists that we

01:16:32.058 --> 01:16:35.781
have, trying to give you a consolidated list of

01:16:35.958 --> 01:16:40.149
all the different projects. And even the one I sent out had some

01:16:40.649 --> 01:16:44.741
spelling errors, which means that the form— formats are not— that

01:16:44.869 --> 01:16:48.336
have been fixed since I've sent it out. aren't sorting exactly

01:16:48.836 --> 01:16:52.251
perfectly. But really, we're trying to consolidate a project, a capital

01:16:52.751 --> 01:16:56.310
project list, projects, initiatives, and even right down to the listening tour

01:16:56.342 --> 01:16:59.968
that you have going on where you're going to various neighborhoods and residents

01:17:00.468 --> 01:17:04.284
are asking for things. Some of those translate into a quick, quick answer,

01:17:04.300 --> 01:17:08.215
and some of those are a major project that we're taking on or potentially

01:17:08.715 --> 01:17:12.530
taking on. And every project out there is has

01:17:12.562 --> 01:17:16.055
the potential to make life better for our residents. And so we

01:17:16.152 --> 01:17:19.773
want to truly look at all of those projects

01:17:19.837 --> 01:17:23.763
and, and, and we want to give them the due diligence needed.

01:17:24.645 --> 01:17:27.433
But the— and sometimes the enemy of, of great is good,

01:17:27.785 --> 01:17:31.519
because we maybe have, um, something that we really, really want

01:17:32.019 --> 01:17:35.958
to accomplish, and then we might get diverted with other things. And so as staff,

01:17:36.038 --> 01:17:39.404
we want to make sure that we're fully aligned with the will of the people,

01:17:39.564 --> 01:17:42.756
which you are their elected representatives. And so we want

01:17:43.256 --> 01:17:45.963
to make sure that we're working at your bequest and doing the things that you

01:17:46.463 --> 01:17:50.212
would have us do. So how does that— I'm just going to go

01:17:50.292 --> 01:17:53.227
really quickly through these first few slides and get right into the projects.

01:17:53.997 --> 01:17:57.043
But how does the city go about doing that? Well, you know,

01:17:57.364 --> 01:18:00.234
we have in the past, and this is more maybe for the sake of the

01:18:00.734 --> 01:18:04.308
public, we in the past have tried to determine what our values

01:18:04.484 --> 01:18:07.726
are as a community. And what we want to provide. And that's, you know,

01:18:07.791 --> 01:18:11.821
a healthy and safe community. We want to have accountability and transparency.

01:18:12.286 --> 01:18:16.413
We want to provide great services for our residents, innovation, make sure we're growing

01:18:16.461 --> 01:18:19.913
in the right way. We want to be efficient with the dollars that

01:18:20.413 --> 01:18:23.590
we're entrusted with, and we want sustainability. And we want to make sure

01:18:24.090 --> 01:18:27.523
our programs can, can be sustainable and not just be one-time, one-off.

01:18:28.422 --> 01:18:31.184
And so when we look at our values, we try to tie that back to

01:18:31.232 --> 01:18:34.507
our strategic goals and what the council would have us work on. And we

01:18:35.007 --> 01:18:37.749
know that you guys are going to— you're all going through a process right now

01:18:37.878 --> 01:18:41.312
to update these. Um, where we have a new council, we're going to be making

01:18:41.409 --> 01:18:45.534
tweaks to this. And so we want to rep— uh, respect the fact that things

01:18:45.582 --> 01:18:48.230
do change over time, and so we need to be, uh, flexible.

01:18:49.129 --> 01:18:52.548
But our current stated goals are really to have a diversified

01:18:53.125 --> 01:18:56.897
economic growth. Anything that we do, we need to be able to have

01:18:57.397 --> 01:18:59.754
the golden goose that be able to keep— have it keep going forward. So we

01:19:00.254 --> 01:19:04.064
don't create an undue burden on our residents. We want to have robust infrastructure

01:19:04.564 --> 01:19:07.685
that, that really can stand the test of time, unique reputational,

01:19:08.038 --> 01:19:11.259
um, evolution. We want to, we want to have

01:19:11.759 --> 01:19:15.152
something different when you come around Unity Pass or coming right into Eagle Mountain

01:19:15.200 --> 01:19:18.694
itself right at the beginning, uh, from Saratoga. You want to feel the

01:19:18.710 --> 01:19:22.555
difference, what it's like to come into Eagle Mountain and feel what Eagle Mountain's

01:19:23.055 --> 01:19:26.545
all about. We want to be proactive and generational in our planning because we

01:19:27.045 --> 01:19:29.030
have kids that we want to be able to retain and keep here in the

01:19:29.530 --> 01:19:31.739
future. And so we look at schools and universities and all the different things,

01:19:32.155 --> 01:19:35.378
job creation, to keep our kids and our grandkids near us at home.

01:19:36.099 --> 01:19:39.529
We want to have interactive environmental stewardship. We don't

01:19:40.029 --> 01:19:43.873
want to damage our home and what we call our

01:19:44.373 --> 01:19:47.544
hometown. And we want to have dynamic services and enhancements. We want to make the

01:19:48.044 --> 01:19:50.686
quality of life better for the residents that we serve. So we start with our

01:19:51.186 --> 01:19:54.439
mission statement, and this is Eagle Mountain's mission statement. That may be tweaked in the

01:19:54.471 --> 01:19:58.405
near future of upcoming retreats. And then from that,

01:19:58.774 --> 01:20:01.873
we've really tried to prioritize the task that you would have us

01:20:02.002 --> 01:20:04.619
do. And that's really done on a basis of,

01:20:05.438 --> 01:20:08.569
first and foremost, there's nothing more important than

01:20:08.649 --> 01:20:11.443
having a strong foundation of public safety.

01:20:12.342 --> 01:20:15.586
That is the foundation. That is the basis. And so

01:20:16.115 --> 01:20:18.909
that's when we look at our public safety as being the most important.

01:20:18.990 --> 01:20:22.233
Important aspect of our community. If we can't go home and have a safe

01:20:22.733 --> 01:20:26.087
community, have police, fire, and EMS response,

01:20:26.552 --> 01:20:29.667
then really we have nothing that really we can offer.

01:20:30.342 --> 01:20:33.473
But on that foundation, we build— we should have a

01:20:33.537 --> 01:20:37.888
balanced approach to providing essential services. That's all the infrastructure

01:20:38.388 --> 01:20:41.967
that we would provide. Economic prosperity so that people have the

01:20:42.467 --> 01:20:45.643
ability to learn and grow and have jobs here in our community. And ultimately,

01:20:45.676 --> 01:20:48.775
we have a great quality of life that we provide our residents. And so when

01:20:48.807 --> 01:20:51.777
we think about all the different tasks that we could take on or all the

01:20:52.277 --> 01:20:55.566
projects, the more that they can cross over and achieve more

01:20:55.615 --> 01:20:58.906
than one thing, the better off we are. So if we take on a project

01:20:59.406 --> 01:21:03.049
that not only enhances our quality of life but attracts new businesses in

01:21:03.193 --> 01:21:07.159
as well, and our essential services that maybe this business brings,

01:21:07.673 --> 01:21:10.631
the infrastructure can support our overall community. Community as well,

01:21:11.051 --> 01:21:14.199
and that creates stability in property taxes that can be provided for our

01:21:14.699 --> 01:21:18.281
public safety, that's a win across the board. And so we look at this

01:21:18.651 --> 01:21:20.808
and we want to make sure that we're doing it and doing it in the

01:21:21.308 --> 01:21:24.897
right way, because sometimes communities get out of balance fairly easily.

01:21:26.971 --> 01:21:30.367
I always have used this slide since the very beginning of being a city manager.

01:21:30.853 --> 01:21:34.395
I am absolutely confident and I have nothing but faith and trust in

01:21:34.427 --> 01:21:37.788
the, in the city staff That we can do anything that you want.

01:21:38.759 --> 01:21:42.192
We have talented people, great people on board that can accomplish

01:21:42.257 --> 01:21:45.708
whatever you would have us accomplish. But we can't do everything. We can do anything,

01:21:45.806 --> 01:21:48.897
but not everything. As soon as we try, that's when we fail, because we're spread

01:21:48.913 --> 01:21:52.285
too thin. We are your engine. You are

01:21:52.785 --> 01:21:55.099
the drivers. You tell us where to go. We will get you there.

01:21:55.276 --> 01:21:58.688
That's the whole ultimate thing. We talked in one of

01:21:59.188 --> 01:22:02.518
our last retreats about financial best practices. need to use budgeting

01:22:02.615 --> 01:22:06.306
as a tool to establish our priorities into tangible dollars so that

01:22:06.806 --> 01:22:11.201
projects really do move forward in a manner that you, the elected leaders, would establish.

01:22:12.004 --> 01:22:15.342
Our, our enterprise funds should be, um, and what are enterprise

01:22:15.455 --> 01:22:19.307
funds for the sake of the residents? An enterprise fund

01:22:19.323 --> 01:22:23.255
is a fund that we consider to be like a business. So our water

01:22:23.755 --> 01:22:27.155
fund supports water, and our water rates should be, uh, set and

01:22:27.300 --> 01:22:30.407
established such that they cover the costs of running that

01:22:30.552 --> 01:22:34.561
enterprise. Sewer for sewer and storm

01:22:35.061 --> 01:22:38.659
drainage for storm drainage, all those different enterprise funds are treated as businesses.

01:22:39.176 --> 01:22:42.750
Our general fund is much larger, and, and that's where we take

01:22:42.831 --> 01:22:44.885
some of our other things like public safety out of.

01:22:46.580 --> 01:22:50.123
I have used this quite a bit, that we can't spend our

01:22:50.220 --> 01:22:52.398
income, we can only spend our income.

01:22:53.166 --> 01:22:57.049
So real dollars is what we can actually plan for. In Eagle

01:22:57.549 --> 01:23:00.771
Mountain, I have learned that there's another term called when-come,

01:23:01.364 --> 01:23:05.520
meaning that if-come is if it translates. But let's

01:23:06.020 --> 01:23:09.482
face it, Eagle Mountain is growing so quickly and we have things being built

01:23:09.611 --> 01:23:12.546
on the fly. We know that they're going to come, and so it is a

01:23:12.579 --> 01:23:16.108
matter of when some of those financial benefits will hit our residents.

01:23:16.670 --> 01:23:20.155
And I truly believe that it's going to transform our We're on a tipping point

01:23:20.606 --> 01:23:24.646
of where we're going as a community, and so long as we create a

01:23:24.662 --> 01:23:28.274
financially stable environment, I believe

01:23:28.774 --> 01:23:32.253
very firmly that Eagle Mountain is going to continue to bloom and prosper with

01:23:32.285 --> 01:23:35.330
great jobs, great opportunities, and quality of

01:23:35.426 --> 01:23:38.841
life like we've never had before. We don't want to spend

01:23:38.922 --> 01:23:42.982
one-time dollars for recurring purposes. We want to create stable,

01:23:43.920 --> 01:23:49.037
sustainable revenue streams streams. So, and we want to tie those most

01:23:49.262 --> 01:23:52.539
stable revenue streams to our most essential services like public safety.

01:23:53.535 --> 01:23:56.909
We want to maintain what we have rather

01:23:57.409 --> 01:24:00.300
than replace. We got to take care of what we own. It's kind of like

01:24:00.800 --> 01:24:03.400
changing the oil in your car. You change the oil in your car so you

01:24:03.900 --> 01:24:06.517
don't have to replace that car, right? So we take care of what we have.

01:24:07.433 --> 01:24:11.193
We should only dip into reserves for emergency purposes. It's better for us to

01:24:11.273 --> 01:24:13.840
own versus rent. So we have to think about that when it comes to,

01:24:13.857 --> 01:24:17.613
like, city facilities. And that the city is like building a house.

01:24:18.371 --> 01:24:21.000
When we build a city, we start with the foundation and we work our way

01:24:21.032 --> 01:24:24.662
up. And many years I've spent in another city

01:24:25.307 --> 01:24:28.986
where all we wanted to talk about was what color

01:24:29.018 --> 01:24:31.052
the house was going to be, or what kind of curtains we were going to

01:24:31.100 --> 01:24:34.856
put up, or how large the kitchen was. But there was nothing more important than

01:24:35.356 --> 01:24:37.495
starting with the foundation and making sure it was stable and strong. Thank you.

01:24:37.863 --> 01:24:41.188
And it had good compaction, and then we start building the walls and the roof

01:24:41.236 --> 01:24:43.870
to make sure it's not getting wet, and we do things in the right order,

01:24:44.368 --> 01:24:47.725
and everything else will take care of itself. When it comes to revenue,

01:24:47.741 --> 01:24:51.323
there's really three main revenues for a city: it's our property taxes,

01:24:51.436 --> 01:24:54.857
our sales taxes, and the fees that we charge. And so we have

01:24:54.889 --> 01:24:58.616
to have balanced and stable revenue streams. And if

01:24:58.744 --> 01:25:02.663
we rely on on things that are changing from

01:25:03.163 --> 01:25:07.003
time to time, like sales tax, then we follow the winds of

01:25:08.150 --> 01:25:11.927
the market and not necessarily the stability that comes from having

01:25:12.447 --> 01:25:16.003
diversification of our revenues. With that said,

01:25:16.036 --> 01:25:19.608
I use the analogy of a house. What is the foundation

01:25:19.657 --> 01:25:23.163
of our house? It really is public safety. From that,

01:25:23.228 --> 01:25:25.555
we have to have our infrastructure, which is our public works,

01:25:26.407 --> 01:25:29.722
and that builds our essential services. From there, we can have our economic

01:25:30.222 --> 01:25:33.597
prosperity. And when it's all said and done, that enhances the quality of life with

01:25:34.097 --> 01:25:36.109
the color of the curtains or the color of the house or whatnot.

01:25:37.980 --> 01:25:41.130
And so all of these tasks, we really need to start looking at

01:25:41.630 --> 01:25:45.148
them, all these potential projects that we're taking on, we need to look at them

01:25:45.648 --> 01:25:48.796
through the prism of what is the most important.

01:25:49.342 --> 01:25:52.925
And oftentimes we have to deal with the urgent. Sometimes the urgent is,

01:25:53.439 --> 01:25:55.962
hey, there's a water leak, a water main blew, and we got to go take

01:25:56.010 --> 01:25:59.902
care of it. But the important is maybe we need

01:26:00.402 --> 01:26:04.111
to be checking every single one of our sewer manholes on a regular

01:26:04.611 --> 01:26:07.517
basis, on a routine, to make sure that we're not— and we're cleaning out our

01:26:08.017 --> 01:26:10.538
sewer mains so we don't have a backup. You know what I mean? That's regular

01:26:11.038 --> 01:26:14.057
maintaining maintenance. That's important. The urgent is

01:26:14.154 --> 01:26:17.658
when it gets blocked. And of course, then we also have things that are not

01:26:18.158 --> 01:26:21.200
important nor urgent, and those are things that can waste or be time wasters.

01:26:21.265 --> 01:26:24.366
Okay. And so when we start looking at that and we want to try

01:26:24.866 --> 01:26:28.838
to become more effective as an organization, we also have to recognize that

01:26:29.338 --> 01:26:33.198
we have certain bandwidth and we also have certain growth that we

01:26:33.698 --> 01:26:37.318
need to do. And so I'm trying to really work hard to build the future

01:26:37.430 --> 01:26:41.358
of our city. We're about a third the size of staff

01:26:41.518 --> 01:26:45.461
as comparable cities of our size. And so I recognize that staff

01:26:45.961 --> 01:26:48.924
that I do have today are the leaders of the future as we continue to

01:26:48.973 --> 01:26:51.661
build more and bring more frontline employees on.

01:26:52.434 --> 01:26:56.266
And so we're doing things like a leadership training

01:26:57.103 --> 01:27:00.407
program right now where we have more than 55 members of our

01:27:00.907 --> 01:27:03.812
staff, either current or future leaders who want to be future leaders,

01:27:04.280 --> 01:27:07.877
learning about management principles and leadership principles and how they

01:27:07.910 --> 01:27:11.524
can better grow and lead their organizations going forward.

01:27:11.945 --> 01:27:15.612
We can't all do it. We've the bottleneck if we don't

01:27:15.628 --> 01:27:18.684
rely and trust on others and let them grow in their roles.

01:27:19.364 --> 01:27:22.824
So we're looking at organizational alignment and policy maturity so we can go from

01:27:23.324 --> 01:27:25.756
a crawl to a walk to a fly. And I— and of course I had

01:27:26.256 --> 01:27:28.806
to put the fly in there because if there's anything I like, it's aviation.

01:27:30.396 --> 01:27:33.815
So what's— what are the major projects? I sent a spreadsheet out to

01:27:34.315 --> 01:27:37.967
the council, and that spreadsheet is actively being worked on by

01:27:38.467 --> 01:27:41.408
our functional area directors and their department heads right now.

01:27:42.292 --> 01:27:45.762
And so it is changing on the fly. But I just wanted

01:27:46.262 --> 01:27:48.639
to review for you and for the public some of those major things that we

01:27:49.139 --> 01:27:52.335
have going on. And I want to talk about how best

01:27:52.495 --> 01:27:56.079
and what your direction would be on how we could prioritize those.

01:27:56.432 --> 01:27:59.887
Because some things, it's going to be challenging to prioritize because

01:28:00.387 --> 01:28:03.502
they're already active. We're already moving forward. So those have been approved in the

01:28:04.002 --> 01:28:07.552
past. Some have the opportunity for

01:28:08.052 --> 01:28:09.811
us to be evaluated on whether we should take them on or not. So I

01:28:10.311 --> 01:28:12.864
just broke down some of the top ones, not all of them, but some of

01:28:13.364 --> 01:28:17.332
the top, uh, projects that our current, um, city staff is wor— working

01:28:17.348 --> 01:28:20.980
on to make enhancements right now to enhance the quality of

01:28:21.480 --> 01:28:24.997
life of our residents. And these are not ranked, these are just thrown on the

01:28:25.497 --> 01:28:29.437
board, and we are looking to our elected leaders and the public in general to

