02:54:21.715 --> 02:54:25.094
Next item. is an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City, Utah,

02:54:25.320 --> 02:54:29.154
amending the master plan known as the Transportation Master

02:54:29.170 --> 02:54:32.315
Plan, the TMP, and the Impact Fee Facilities Plan.

02:54:33.785 --> 02:54:36.533
I know David Salazar has done a lot of work on this, and we'll turn

02:54:37.033 --> 02:54:39.938
it to you. Thanks, Mayor, council members.

02:54:40.072 --> 02:54:43.367
We're glad to be here. So the

02:54:43.867 --> 02:54:47.448
purpose of today's item is that we are bringing the Transportation Master Plan back

02:54:47.948 --> 02:54:51.082
in the IFP. Back for review. We're going to try and do these on a

02:54:51.227 --> 02:54:54.520
year and a half to 2-year basis. The last

02:54:55.020 --> 02:54:57.716
year was particularly busy. That's why we're coming back with some pretty big changes.

02:54:57.812 --> 02:55:00.703
We didn't want to wait another year and a half, 2 years to capture.

02:55:01.940 --> 02:55:05.827
So some of the summary will be updating some of the current transportation priorities

02:55:05.843 --> 02:55:09.200
and plans, updated roadway names, classifications,

02:55:10.180 --> 02:55:13.777
revised roadway cross-sections. We did update the modeling

02:55:13.809 --> 02:55:16.878
as well. As well as the current costs

02:55:17.055 --> 02:55:20.460
and contributions throughout, so we can get a more accurate transportation impact fee.

02:55:24.486 --> 02:55:28.743
Excuse me. So I sent you guys an updated list,

02:55:28.824 --> 02:55:32.055
kind of a summary of the updates we made, the major updates that's in your

02:55:32.555 --> 02:55:36.004
packet. We're going to go through each one of them, and then I

02:55:36.504 --> 02:55:39.711
did throw in a couple slides throughout. We can also go through some topics as

02:55:40.211 --> 02:55:43.543
well. And focus on some key projects. So the first main update

02:55:44.043 --> 02:55:47.464
we did was update the priority project list. In the past project

02:55:47.964 --> 02:55:51.467
list, we had the old Mid Valley Road in there as number

02:55:51.967 --> 02:55:54.991
one. We we were very successful, and now that's a UDOT facility, so we no

02:55:55.491 --> 02:55:58.838
longer need that as our number one priority because we have succeeded. Now we are

02:55:59.338 --> 02:56:02.010
still pushing that as much as we can as a city to get that moved

02:56:02.042 --> 02:56:04.960
up in the RTP, as well as MAG funding.

02:56:05.173 --> 02:56:08.433
So that's something we are actively working on. MAG is currently going through

02:56:08.933 --> 02:56:12.499
the RTP prioritization process now, and we've been—

02:56:12.612 --> 02:56:15.970
I was on the working group, Mayor and I both were. And so

02:56:16.019 --> 02:56:19.805
we are heavily giving input on how they are prioritizing projects

02:56:20.210 --> 02:56:24.028
because we are trying to let them know, hey, growth is a huge component for

02:56:24.528 --> 02:56:27.994
us. It is huge, and it's been overlooked in western Utah County.

02:56:28.625 --> 02:56:31.363
And so they— I feel like they're finally hearing us now, and they're understanding the

02:56:31.863 --> 02:56:34.721
growth that we have and the needs and the demands that we have in our

02:56:34.737 --> 02:56:38.513
area. So that's a huge one. The other

02:56:39.013 --> 02:56:42.514
major change to this was we added the Silver Lake projects, were pulled up the

02:56:43.014 --> 02:56:46.097
list, and those are the 2— that's line item number 4.

02:56:47.029 --> 02:56:49.937
Those are connections to Saratoga Springs,

02:56:50.644 --> 02:56:53.793
and those have been on our transportation master plan since before I was here.

02:56:54.741 --> 02:56:57.327
But with the new high school coming online in fall of 2024,

02:56:58.420 --> 02:57:01.537
We are pulling those up on the priority list, and so

02:57:01.553 --> 02:57:03.674
I want to talk a little bit about those connections before we go on to

02:57:03.722 --> 02:57:06.839
the next item. So we did a study with

02:57:07.498 --> 02:57:11.032
Hales Engineering, and they came back with the intersection at Golden

02:57:11.532 --> 02:57:15.403
Eagle and Silver Lake Parkway warranting a traffic signal for the movements that occurred

02:57:15.903 --> 02:57:19.146
in that intersection. Their recommendations including removing the

02:57:19.646 --> 02:57:21.508
roundabout and adding a traffic signal there,

02:57:22.552 --> 02:57:25.782
moving all the crossing to. To the controlled intersection. Currently right

02:57:26.282 --> 02:57:28.536
now, the north crossing is at Golden Medal, further to the north,

02:57:29.152 --> 02:57:33.018
and the recommendation would be to pull everything down to the intersection with crossing

02:57:33.050 --> 02:57:36.661
guards. And then also, they're looking at the

02:57:36.742 --> 02:57:40.513
east leg of the intersection to realign it to a more conventional intersection.

02:57:41.431 --> 02:57:45.586
Currently, that leg is narrow and skewed, and that can create visibility issues

02:57:45.940 --> 02:57:49.530
when aligning with intersections, as well as limitations in site

02:57:49.578 --> 02:57:53.552
visibility. So here's 2 photos I

02:57:53.616 --> 02:57:56.460
have of that intersection. This is looking eastward.

02:57:57.540 --> 02:58:00.928
The photo on the left is current conditions, and the one on the right is

02:58:01.106 --> 02:58:03.769
AI-generated what those improvements would look like.

02:58:04.689 --> 02:58:08.532
So the, the concerns that were brought up last week from Councilmember Wood was

02:58:09.032 --> 02:58:12.169
we'd be pushing traffic closer to pedestrians, and it's a very valid concern. And that's

02:58:12.669 --> 02:58:14.448
why I wanted to bring this up to you guys so you could have a

02:58:14.948 --> 02:58:16.808
chance to understand the intentions of this project.

02:58:17.836 --> 02:58:21.994
So the, the dimension of the edge of pavement to trail would be maintained.

02:58:22.428 --> 02:58:26.442
We would be realigning that trail in that right-of-way.

02:58:27.148 --> 02:58:30.022
The parking lot would stay as is, and we would take out some of the

02:58:30.038 --> 02:58:32.671
area in between the edge of trail and the parking lot.

02:58:33.731 --> 02:58:37.889
Also, there'd be a— with the wider pavement, we'd now go to a 36-foot wide

02:58:38.403 --> 02:58:41.569
width, so we'd have room for a shoulder. As well,

02:58:42.327 --> 02:58:45.971
which— and potential on-street parking, which would actually push traffic further

02:58:46.471 --> 02:58:50.363
away from the trail. This, again, this 90-degree alignment provides

02:58:50.509 --> 02:58:53.130
better visibility for turning movements and pedestrians.

02:58:55.575 --> 02:58:58.788
So the other intersection was a little further north at Silver

02:58:59.288 --> 02:59:02.993
Lake, Campbell, and Woodhaven. While this intersection was

02:59:03.025 --> 02:59:05.326
not a full warrant based on the turning movements,

02:59:06.114 --> 02:59:09.541
staff and the consultant did identify concerns from pedestrian crossings,

02:59:10.134 --> 02:59:13.499
with the recommendations to improve this intersection. This would also

02:59:13.999 --> 02:59:17.569
have a traffic signal, um, and then Silver Lake Parkway would be

02:59:17.585 --> 02:59:20.358
realigned to tee and terminate into Woodhaven.

02:59:20.935 --> 02:59:24.797
Woodhaven would then continue north and south, and then the southern portion

02:59:25.297 --> 02:59:27.216
would be Woodhaven going forward.

02:59:28.707 --> 02:59:32.296
Um, Campbell Circle, as, as Brett is well— Councilmember Wright is well

02:59:32.328 --> 02:59:36.222
aware of, Campbell Circle would be limited

02:59:36.722 --> 02:59:39.202
limited access with a crash gate for fire requirements, and Campbell Circuit would have to

02:59:39.234 --> 02:59:42.899
access Silver Lake Parkway from the west a few hundred feet with their other access.

02:59:44.500 --> 02:59:47.356
And again, this would help with the pedestrian crossings in the area.

02:59:49.320 --> 02:59:51.740
Excuse me.

02:59:52.402 --> 02:59:56.114
Update number 2 is Lake Mountain Corridor. So this is a screenshot from our

02:59:56.614 --> 02:59:59.906
future classification map. It's nice seeing the 2 big blue lines on there. So we

03:00:00.406 --> 03:00:02.806
now have 2 major UDOT facilities coming to our region.

03:00:03.255 --> 03:00:06.788
Cedar Valley Highway, which peels off of SR 73

03:00:07.288 --> 03:00:10.593
in the north and south direction, and that east and west is Lakemound Corridor,

03:00:11.123 --> 03:00:14.591
which is currently called Mid-Valley Road. Now this is going to

03:00:15.091 --> 03:00:18.734
be a UDOT facility. UDOT currently has a Mid-Valley Road in

03:00:18.862 --> 03:00:22.298
Tooele County, and so we needed a new name. They originally

03:00:22.798 --> 03:00:26.409
assigned us the name Crown Ridge Crest, I think it was, and not Nobody liked

03:00:26.909 --> 03:00:29.877
that name, so council gave us some good direction on what to name it,

03:00:31.370 --> 03:00:34.887
right? They gave some good direction, and MAG and UDOT are

03:00:35.387 --> 03:00:38.532
both on board. That name is on the RTP right now, and it's been identified.

03:00:38.612 --> 03:00:42.546
So that is the new name of that road. We are working on getting new

03:00:43.046 --> 03:00:47.170
signage for Mid Valley and working with Frontier Middle School, the seminary

03:00:47.670 --> 03:00:50.831
building, and then the fire station. I forget what UAF station is over there.

03:00:51.345 --> 03:00:54.611
Getting those folks readdressed. Once we get the new signs ordered,

03:00:55.018 --> 03:00:56.368
we will get them readdressed accordingly.

03:00:59.290 --> 03:01:03.251
Update number 3 was just catching typos throughout the project. We found some inconsistencies,

03:01:03.283 --> 03:01:06.760
some old references. This was an old document that had

03:01:07.260 --> 03:01:10.871
been kind of updated throughout the years. Our next major update, we're going to start

03:01:11.371 --> 03:01:15.469
from scratch. We're going to have an all-new document. We've seen other cities' TMPs,

03:01:15.614 --> 03:01:18.544
and they're a lot shorter and a lot So we're going to go to something

03:01:19.044 --> 03:01:22.526
a little bit different going forward, kind of start from scratch. So don't threaten

03:01:22.574 --> 03:01:26.251
us with a good time, David. You don't want to read 200 pages?

03:01:26.363 --> 03:01:30.056
You know, come on. It's a good document, but I told Vince

03:01:30.556 --> 03:01:33.476
it reads like a book report, so it's, it's tough to follow sometimes.

03:01:33.749 --> 03:01:37.153
So we want something that, you know, our residents can follow well,

03:01:38.229 --> 03:01:41.103
development, it's easy to follow to look for the information that you need.

03:01:43.929 --> 03:01:47.494
Update number 4, we updated the freeway and expressway criteria

03:01:47.959 --> 03:01:51.861
in the functional classification. Before, so that first column,

03:01:51.974 --> 03:01:55.522
we had the spacing of the traffic movement at 4 miles and it was

03:01:55.570 --> 03:01:58.830
high speed only. With the addition of Lake Mountain Corridor,

03:01:59.103 --> 03:02:02.892
we needed to lower that in the development regions because we

03:02:02.908 --> 03:02:06.473
know Lake Mountain Corridor is going to go through city center and then some

03:02:06.505 --> 03:02:09.660
residential up in the mountains as well. But the majority of that is going to

03:02:09.709 --> 03:02:13.200
be, you know, we're prioritizing as a high-speed connector, but we still

03:02:13.700 --> 03:02:17.645
have to cover the areas that are not. And again, our goal for

03:02:18.145 --> 03:02:21.880
this is going to be a limited access, you know, high-functioning road, but still within

03:02:22.380 --> 03:02:23.617
some development we will have some connections.

03:02:26.078 --> 03:02:29.874
Update number 5 is the roadway cross-sections. Our old

03:02:30.374 --> 03:02:33.605
cross-sections had, you know, those beautiful raised landscape medians throughout on

03:02:34.105 --> 03:02:37.069
the major collector, minor arterial, and the major arterial.

03:02:38.449 --> 03:02:42.013
The new sections have removed those landscaped medians

03:02:42.559 --> 03:02:45.818
to improve U-turn flexibility, to remove the

03:02:45.834 --> 03:02:50.153
hazards we've seen from accidents from maintenance vehicles working

03:02:50.265 --> 03:02:54.166
in the travel lanes. It also reduces blind spots at intersections

03:02:54.439 --> 03:02:58.292
and provides additional capacity for turning lanes. And I

03:02:58.372 --> 03:03:03.108
spoke with our arborist. Even though we're going to be removing some of these landscape

03:03:03.608 --> 03:03:04.818
We are going to be working on kind of beautifying our park strips and adding

03:03:05.318 --> 03:03:08.745
more greenage because we want to maintain that Eagle Mountain feel and not turn this

03:03:09.245 --> 03:03:13.032
into a concrete jungle. So that's a key priority

03:03:13.532 --> 03:03:17.125
we're working on, especially along Pony Express and Ranches. Those are our 2 areas

03:03:17.157 --> 03:03:20.166
we don't want to lose that green. I'm from the Midwest, so I like green.

03:03:20.750 --> 03:03:23.515
So the sage is okay, but I like the green personally.

03:03:24.946 --> 03:03:28.451
So here's an image. Of those 3

03:03:28.951 --> 03:03:32.065
sections, the top one didn't change. We just redid it with great help from our

03:03:32.081 --> 03:03:35.602
communications department. So we

03:03:36.102 --> 03:03:40.155
have added a turning— center turning lane in the major collector, the minor arterial,

03:03:40.220 --> 03:03:41.172
and the major arterial.

03:03:44.789 --> 03:03:48.034
We've also added a new roadway classification, the principal arterial.

03:03:48.679 --> 03:03:51.359
And this is to help with some of the confusion, because before we had 2

03:03:51.859 --> 03:03:55.169
major arterials. One was a five-lane, one was a seven-lane. And looking at the map,

03:03:55.185 --> 03:03:59.148
it could be confusing to understand which is what right-of-way, what's the lane configuration.

03:03:59.918 --> 03:04:03.640
So we took away the old park strip, a parkway cross-section, which was

03:04:03.816 --> 03:04:07.571
a four-lane road with large landscape medians and no shoulders.

03:04:08.485 --> 03:04:12.512
This will be a seven-lane arterial, and it

03:04:13.012 --> 03:04:16.186
has two right-of-ways: 176 for new developments,

03:04:16.587 --> 03:04:20.045
and then the 206 is for the existing. Existing preserved right-of-way along

03:04:20.144 --> 03:04:21.802
Pony Express and Ranches Parkway.

03:04:23.880 --> 03:04:25.979
And it makes it again just easier when we go to the overall map.

03:04:26.028 --> 03:04:29.484
You'll see the, the line work is just easier to follow. You know, it works

03:04:29.564 --> 03:04:33.773
better for planning and development. So now

03:04:34.273 --> 03:04:36.991
we're going to talk a little bit about Ranches Parkway, as this was a very

03:04:37.491 --> 03:04:41.180
important project. It's one of our, it's one of our busiest corridors in the

03:04:41.680 --> 03:04:45.444
whole city, as we know. So Ranches Parkway is 4 lanes with a very

03:04:45.476 --> 03:04:48.269
large 37-foot median in the middle.

03:04:49.087 --> 03:04:52.586
We've had major traffic congestion along the way. Speeding is

03:04:52.618 --> 03:04:56.358
an issue. Enforcement is limited because of the limited turn—

03:04:56.454 --> 03:05:00.723
U-turn movements throughout the corridor. There's no protected pedestrian

03:05:01.223 --> 03:05:04.688
crossings from Pony Express to SR 73— well,

03:05:04.832 --> 03:05:08.828
excuse me, until you hit Stonebridge just before the Maverick. We've also

03:05:09.328 --> 03:05:12.601
had multiple crashes in this corridor. And so

03:05:13.083 --> 03:05:16.342
with this project, we're looking to improve in 2 phases. So phase

03:05:16.375 --> 03:05:20.501
1, we're looking to remove the landscape medians and add 1 additional

03:05:20.533 --> 03:05:24.387
travel lane in each direction and a center turn lane. With this

03:05:24.887 --> 03:05:28.642
project, we're also looking to add 2 traffic signals, kind of about

03:05:29.142 --> 03:05:32.560
third points throughout the corridor, one at Half Mile and the other at Braxton,

03:05:33.635 --> 03:05:36.706
and then limit the other access. On the parkway,

03:05:37.418 --> 03:05:41.316
and those accesses will either be ride-in ride-outs or three-quarter. And for our residents,

03:05:41.380 --> 03:05:44.999
a three-quarter is a ride-in ride-out or left-in because

03:05:45.257 --> 03:05:48.724
left-outs are your longest turning movements and often are the most

03:05:49.224 --> 03:05:52.421
hazardous and time-consuming. Where a left-in, you face traffic and you

03:05:52.921 --> 03:05:56.565
often have momentum to make your turn in. The other accesses can

03:05:57.278 --> 03:06:00.030
reach those traffic signals and still make their protected left-outs.

03:06:01.719 --> 03:06:05.509
Again, we're going to beautify the park strips and add

03:06:06.009 --> 03:06:09.235
more trees and shrubs throughout. And with this, we're going to improve

03:06:09.267 --> 03:06:13.057
the intersection at Pony Express and Ranches. As you

03:06:13.557 --> 03:06:16.943
know, that intersection is probably one of our worst intersections in the city. We're looking

03:06:17.443 --> 03:06:21.055
to add dual left turn lanes heading east to north,

03:06:21.729 --> 03:06:25.021
and then we're going to add dual right lanes south to west.

03:06:25.985 --> 03:06:28.704
And then every leg will have a dedicated right turn lane. Turn lane as we

03:06:28.784 --> 03:06:32.592
overall widen the intersection. So the other challenge of Pony Express and

03:06:33.092 --> 03:06:35.336
Ranches is that when you need to slow down and make a turn, there's no

03:06:35.400 --> 03:06:39.216
turn lane. So you now are impeding all your traffic who's already congested

03:06:39.716 --> 03:06:43.054
anyway. So we will be having, you know, dedicated turn pockets for all those movements.

03:06:44.072 --> 03:06:47.422
And this summer we are doing construction on the north end of that project at

03:06:47.454 --> 03:06:51.335
73 and Ranches. We're widening that to

03:06:51.835 --> 03:06:54.885
6 lanes, so we'll have 2 left turn lanes, 2 throughs and 2 rights.

03:06:55.631 --> 03:06:59.486
And then we're adding a signal at Campus Drive, a high tee, so vehicles can

03:06:59.534 --> 03:07:02.454
exit that safely. That's Phase 1.

03:07:04.407 --> 03:07:07.570
Phase 2 is going to come 5 to 15 years later. We're not

03:07:07.635 --> 03:07:10.798
quite sure on that timing yet, but that will come when the new sewer trunk

03:07:11.298 --> 03:07:14.606
line is needed. From this graphic here, you can see it's under

03:07:15.106 --> 03:07:18.612
the shoulder. I don't have a laser pointer, but it's, it's under the, the far

03:07:19.407 --> 03:07:22.975
Right shoulder. And so for that Phase 2, we'll kick out the curb

03:07:23.475 --> 03:07:26.784
about 10 feet to provide a shoulder for emergency vehicles being pulled

03:07:26.800 --> 03:07:30.769
over, maintenance, or other emergencies. And then we'll install that new sewer

03:07:31.269 --> 03:07:34.915
trunk line out there. And then there's parts of the trail that

03:07:35.415 --> 03:07:38.621
run up against the curb, so we'll adjust those back so the curb— the

03:07:39.121 --> 03:07:41.359
trail is not right up on the curb and there's still park strip in between,

03:07:41.812 --> 03:07:45.733
so the pedestrians have a buffer there. And this also improves safety because

03:07:46.189 --> 03:07:50.021
Adding a shoulder is critical for vehicles who are not paying attention

03:07:50.053 --> 03:07:53.128
to the road, and this way they don't get ejected off the road and drive

03:07:53.628 --> 03:07:55.978
into the park strip. So that's another safety feature we're adding.

03:07:57.970 --> 03:08:01.270
So Phase 1, I took to MAG earlier this year for

03:08:01.383 --> 03:08:05.328
the 2016 TIP selection, and we competed

03:08:05.828 --> 03:08:09.290
with other cities for funding, and we made it through the concept

03:08:09.790 --> 03:08:13.814
and the idea cycle. And we were shortlisted 4th out

03:08:14.314 --> 03:08:17.905
of the cities for funding. This funding selection is scheduled

03:08:18.405 --> 03:08:21.851
to be awarded next Thursday at the MPO board meeting.

03:08:22.300 --> 03:08:26.231
It's already gone through TAC and been recommended. And so if

03:08:26.731 --> 03:08:30.450
that's the case, this project will be a funded project through MAG in '29-'30.

03:08:30.563 --> 03:08:33.916
Funds will be available. And so the current funds are

03:08:34.416 --> 03:08:37.477
$11.8 million with future funds inflated.

03:08:39.028 --> 03:08:42.204
13.8. And that is the largest project that the city will be

03:08:42.704 --> 03:08:43.435
awarded by MAG to date.

03:08:46.090 --> 03:08:49.368
So some of the concerns we had from the residents who live

03:08:49.868 --> 03:08:52.549
in the ranches was, hey, can we— you know, not going to the meetings,

03:08:52.985 --> 03:08:56.508
you know, can we maybe put our efforts and look at working on

03:08:56.540 --> 03:08:59.838
Airport Road? Is that an option? Will that be able to kind of save the

03:08:59.854 --> 03:09:02.409
ranches as it is or prolong it as long as we can?

03:09:03.409 --> 03:09:06.524
And so we looked at that as we're updating our modeling. This is the latest

03:09:06.588 --> 03:09:09.799
model of the existing level of service, and we have the

03:09:10.299 --> 03:09:14.584
Airport Road in there with 2 lanes. Currently, Ranches Parkway is

03:09:14.712 --> 03:09:18.277
at 29,100, which is just shy of failing

03:09:18.727 --> 03:09:22.243
at 31,400. Before,

03:09:22.259 --> 03:09:25.438
it was failing at 33,300. So Airport did save

03:09:25.470 --> 03:09:29.371
us a little bit, but if we look at the future, It does

03:09:29.547 --> 03:09:33.056
fail pretty quickly, actually. And we do know at the intersections

03:09:33.104 --> 03:09:36.886
it's already failing. So this is a no-build situation.

03:09:37.175 --> 03:09:40.684
We leave Airport Road at 2 lanes, Ranches at 4, just to show how bad

03:09:41.184 --> 03:09:45.508
it could be. The worst along Ranches is at 39,900,

03:09:45.812 --> 03:09:48.056
which is severely failing at that point.

03:09:48.168 --> 03:09:51.998
And then the ask from the residents was if

03:09:52.498 --> 03:09:56.288
we widen Airport Road first to 5 lanes, What does that do for the ranches?

03:09:56.401 --> 03:09:59.784
And we just do the intersection improvements. Well, if you

03:10:00.284 --> 03:10:03.438
look at the, uh, the image here, it actually makes it fail more because we've

03:10:03.938 --> 03:10:08.095
now essentially relieved that bottleneck that is the southern connection and

03:10:08.595 --> 03:10:10.246
allowed more traffic to flow onto ranches.

03:10:12.380 --> 03:10:15.427
So if you look at what we're projecting for a build-out in 2035,

03:10:16.770 --> 03:10:20.142
Ranches Parkway at a 7-lane would have additional capacity

03:10:20.565 --> 03:10:23.955
from the induced demand, which you get, which is normal. But that puts us at

03:10:24.455 --> 03:10:27.701
about 45,900. And that still leaves us with about,

03:10:28.153 --> 03:10:33.469
with some capacity left over. A 7-lane maxes out at 50,500.

03:10:33.968 --> 03:10:37.624
So still having capacity until we start seeing the Cedar Valley Airport come

03:10:37.672 --> 03:10:42.470
on, which will help divert some of this traffic as we approach the 2040-ish,

03:10:42.698 --> 03:10:43.888
2045 timeline.

03:10:48.116 --> 03:10:51.376
So Spring Run, as we've been seeing development kind of

03:10:51.876 --> 03:10:54.844
getting interest and traction in this area, we spent a lot of time with the

03:10:55.344 --> 03:10:59.003
folks out there and working back and forth. Their engineers sent

03:10:59.503 --> 03:11:03.739
through a few different transportation impact studies, and we have come up with

03:11:04.076 --> 03:11:07.721
the, the best layout that we could for the region to service

03:11:07.930 --> 03:11:12.088
as a third connection kind of from the city. Case there's ever issues

03:11:12.169 --> 03:11:15.860
on 73. And so this would hopefully get us additional

03:11:16.360 --> 03:11:19.311
connections to the Wildflower development there. Um,

03:11:20.145 --> 03:11:23.788
it's something we're actually working with Saratoga Springs as this area develops.

03:11:24.286 --> 03:11:26.886
It's, it's going to go from west to east, as we know,

03:11:26.950 --> 03:11:30.545
just as the development is occurring and as they're mining out there. But that's something

03:11:30.706 --> 03:11:33.819
we're actively looking at, and we want to stay ahead of it so we can

03:11:33.835 --> 03:11:36.981
kind of not design things after the fact. We're trying to get ahead of

03:11:37.481 --> 03:11:39.108
things in the city and build for the future.

03:11:41.410 --> 03:11:44.517
So with these maps and new layouts, we went and ran new modeling just so

03:11:45.017 --> 03:11:48.655
we could understand the better the traffic flow. This helps us understand the prioritization of

03:11:48.671 --> 03:11:52.494
projects at the staff level and what's needed in the future

03:11:52.559 --> 03:11:56.925
at 2035, in the near future, or at build-out or at 2050.

03:11:59.189 --> 03:12:01.800
And then from that, that helps us update the project list as well.

03:12:05.787 --> 03:12:09.415
Then we updated the future functional— sorry, the functional classification map.

03:12:09.977 --> 03:12:12.948
This is that line work I was talking about that helps us kind of easier

03:12:13.448 --> 03:12:17.042
to determine which roadway is what. And there is an addendum

03:12:17.107 --> 03:12:20.013
I do need to add into motion if we can, if, if it gets to

03:12:20.513 --> 03:12:23.802
that point. There was a line that was dropped off at East 1000 North.

03:12:23.866 --> 03:12:26.901
I've got a better image of that I will show later if we can include

03:12:27.401 --> 03:12:30.171
that. There was a line that didn't index All the way, so I will show

03:12:30.671 --> 03:12:34.702
that in a little bit. We then went up and updated the project

03:12:35.202 --> 03:12:39.067
cost. As we went through the project, we found that our consultant had

03:12:39.567 --> 03:12:43.352
a lot of money kind of delineated to MAG and some developers. And while

03:12:43.852 --> 03:12:46.282
that's great, and while we would love to have more money from them, like we

03:12:46.314 --> 03:12:49.341
can't always count on these things because we know the MAG cycling is every two

03:12:49.438 --> 03:12:52.601
years, and there's no guarantee of funds. And so, as a city, we have to

03:12:52.700 --> 03:12:55.958
plan for these things and. Kind of a worst-case scenario and see how can we

03:12:55.974 --> 03:12:59.362
fund these roads effectively. And we're always trying to look for

03:12:59.458 --> 03:13:02.846
ways to partner and work with MAG and other entities, but at the

03:13:03.346 --> 03:13:06.002
end of the day, we have to make sure we're capturing cost accurately so we

03:13:06.502 --> 03:13:09.241
can have an accurate transportation impact fee.

03:13:11.731 --> 03:13:14.706
So that's kind of the overall effects of the update, what we were going through,

03:13:14.770 --> 03:13:18.278
trying to be consistent to get things up to date as best as

03:13:18.327 --> 03:13:20.996
we could as a snapshot. You know, this is like trying to hit a moving

03:13:21.028 --> 03:13:24.354
target. It's always updating every day. There's always something new going on.

03:13:24.483 --> 03:13:27.311
But at one point we have to just say this is it. We have to

03:13:27.359 --> 03:13:30.740
present to you our plan in its current form to hopefully get

03:13:31.240 --> 03:13:33.836
it approved and then, you know, keep working on things in the future.

03:13:35.689 --> 03:13:39.729
David, just for clarification for me, you said that we needed to add

03:13:39.906 --> 03:13:44.188
East 1000 North. Yeah, so it's in the recommendation.

03:13:44.253 --> 03:13:47.932
OK, that's what I want on the recommendation. So staff recommends City Council,

03:13:47.980 --> 03:13:50.918
you know, adopt this with this caveat, adding this line.

03:13:51.432 --> 03:13:54.403
There's some major development going down south kind of next to the Meta,

03:13:55.206 --> 03:13:58.353
and this road right here, it's, it's not really a prescriptive road.

03:13:58.417 --> 03:14:01.725
It's just used as a utility access road kind of under the power corridor.

03:14:02.110 --> 03:14:05.674
But we would love to see that as the collector kind of connecting Pony Express

03:14:05.964 --> 03:14:09.512
and our East Expressway. Agreed. And then, and then I have 2 more questions,

03:14:09.560 --> 03:14:13.365
Mayor, if I may. As far as the road sections

03:14:13.381 --> 03:14:14.901
that are contained in the TMP,

03:14:17.790 --> 03:14:20.808
what I'm really wondering is, do we need to update those if we should we

03:14:21.308 --> 03:14:24.537
choose to adopt this? Do we need to update those in our other section of

03:14:24.602 --> 03:14:28.054
code? I believe we probably will want to so that they're so they

03:14:28.554 --> 03:14:32.143
match. We have road classifications as

03:14:32.224 --> 03:14:35.272
part of I don't know if it's sixteen or seventeen. It's in one of those

03:14:35.305 --> 03:14:38.760
code sections. I just. I'll speak into the mic. I know nobody can hear

03:14:39.260 --> 03:14:42.720
me. I'm getting some shakes from the back. I'm just wondering if

03:14:43.220 --> 03:14:46.620
we need to bring back a different section of code that identifies the

03:14:47.120 --> 03:14:49.320
road sections. We're going to need to update that if we go with this,

