the described— Yeah, but they're contained in the code section that talks about road classification.

03:15:01.160 --> 03:15:04.765
So I just think we're going to want them to match. Okay, we'll do

03:15:05.265 --> 03:15:08.888
that then. And then, uh, let's see, and did we contact

03:15:09.388 --> 03:15:12.041
or have any interaction with the Wildlife Alliance? Did they get a chance to review

03:15:12.171 --> 03:15:15.664
the conflict map? That's the last page in this TMP.

03:15:16.230 --> 03:15:19.286
We didn't update that at all from this latest go-round. So, so it

03:15:19.786 --> 03:15:21.743
didn't change? It didn't change. That's what I wanted to know. Thank you.

03:15:23.568 --> 03:15:27.217
One question for me with the Lake Mountain Corridor. In our combined

03:15:27.717 --> 03:15:31.082
meeting that we They mentioned that UDOT would

03:15:31.098 --> 03:15:35.090
not be building the road. Do you want to just talk to

03:15:35.590 --> 03:15:39.354
that point? It made it feel like they're going to take it over after we

03:15:39.854 --> 03:15:43.538
build it, kind of a scenario. So I don't have a great answer

03:15:44.038 --> 03:15:47.626
for that. I mean, because I've heard conflicting information on that, to be perfectly honest.

03:15:48.027 --> 03:15:51.217
I've heard they are, and I've read the code myself, and that is exactly

03:15:51.281 --> 03:15:54.696
how it's written. But I think there's other things at play. On how that's going

03:15:55.196 --> 03:15:58.433
to go, I think this is this kind of road adoption is unique. I don't

03:15:58.933 --> 03:16:02.394
think it's ever been written in this way before. So I suspect this is kind

03:16:02.894 --> 03:16:05.874
of the first step to them fully taking it over. But again, I don't have

03:16:06.374 --> 03:16:08.648
specifics on that. I know that's something we're working with our lobbyist as well,

03:16:08.856 --> 03:16:12.384
and Stephanie and some of the other folks to

03:16:12.884 --> 03:16:16.297
get some clarification so that that language is clear. So okay,

03:16:17.660 --> 03:16:19.729
just a little insight. That was one of the things that I did in.

03:16:19.890 --> 03:16:23.617
I saw like a couple weeks ago, I was talking with Mike Schultz,

03:16:24.260 --> 03:16:26.461
and I think the key— and I was working with Stephanie on this as well—

03:16:27.296 --> 03:16:31.078
it's more about getting it on the funding, which is a long

03:16:31.175 --> 03:16:34.839
ways away. And once it's funded, even if we build it, then they pay for

03:16:35.339 --> 03:16:38.485
it. It's— I think it's more whether or not it's an

03:16:38.581 --> 03:16:42.526
internal project for them to build themselves or subcontract

03:16:43.026 --> 03:16:45.489
it. And I think if we pre-build it,

03:16:46.157 --> 03:16:49.868
If they fund it to a 2032, 2033 type

03:16:50.832 --> 03:16:54.365
programming, then we'd likely move that forward

03:16:54.865 --> 03:16:57.578
by funding it or building it with a subcontractor and then getting reimbursed just like

03:16:58.078 --> 03:17:01.803
we do with MAG. So something of that nature is what we're working on,

03:17:01.851 --> 03:17:05.674
or at least proposing. So I think it's a combination

03:17:06.174 --> 03:17:08.790
of both, right? Yeah.

03:17:09.786 --> 03:17:13.397
So any other questions? Go ahead. Great presentation.

03:17:14.218 --> 03:17:17.949
Yeah, thank you, David. That's really helpful. It's a long document,

03:17:17.998 --> 03:17:21.295
222 pages, so if you can shorten that up a few,

03:17:21.311 --> 03:17:24.482
that would be helpful. Gladly. So this is just more

03:17:24.982 --> 03:17:28.376
of a question on the flexibility

03:17:28.473 --> 03:17:32.271
as we kind of move forward. And really, Ranches Parkway sticks

03:17:32.771 --> 03:17:35.115
out in my mind because I was at that same meeting, and I agree,

03:17:35.131 --> 03:17:38.576
it probably needs to be widened out 3 lanes on both sides. Is there any

03:17:38.672 --> 03:17:42.140
flexibility in design where you have the ability

03:17:42.640 --> 03:17:45.448
to do U-turns, you can narrow the median? I think the medians right now are—

03:17:45.914 --> 03:17:49.045
they're built up way too high, they're way too wide, they don't get watered,

03:17:49.125 --> 03:17:52.336
they look terrible. I don't know how many trees we have in there. We could

03:17:52.836 --> 03:17:56.655
probably relocate or rehome a bunch of those trees, but I'm curious if

03:17:57.155 --> 03:18:00.429
there's any flexibility in design with what we're voting on tonight

03:18:00.445 --> 03:18:04.508
to retain some median To retain the character when

03:18:04.540 --> 03:18:08.620
you enter our city versus turning into the city past

03:18:09.120 --> 03:18:12.861
the Maverick and just seeing, you know, a big open asphalt field,

03:18:12.893 --> 03:18:16.153
'cause it's a pretty straight shot. So my question to you is,

03:18:16.603 --> 03:18:19.928
is there flexibility in the design? Are we adopting this kind of as

03:18:20.041 --> 03:18:22.643
is? So I think, I mean, we could look at that.

03:18:23.655 --> 03:18:26.915
Staff is recommending to get away with the medians from a few reasons.

03:18:28.137 --> 03:18:31.169
You know, we talked about the U-turns and things like that, but there's also other

03:18:31.444 --> 03:18:34.008
questions, concerns I didn't bring up, like water intrusion.

03:18:34.508 --> 03:18:38.308
When you have these medians, you know, you're watering that area, the subgrade becomes soft.

03:18:38.775 --> 03:18:42.446
Ranches and Pony have had more maintenance than a typical road section because that water

03:18:42.881 --> 03:18:45.948
percolates through and it affects the subgrade to the adjacent roadway.

03:18:46.354 --> 03:18:49.717
And so it eventually, it affects the crown of your road. There's other factors

03:18:50.217 --> 03:18:53.859
as well versus kind of capping off and having a proper— crown

03:18:53.923 --> 03:18:57.247
profile of the road, if you will. And so I think the consensus has been

03:18:57.455 --> 03:19:00.843
just to get away from that. The maintenance has been a huge issue. There have

03:19:00.891 --> 03:19:04.408
been accidents of workers being hit, residents being hit as well

03:19:04.520 --> 03:19:08.020
from this. And so I feel like we've outgrown that as

03:19:08.052 --> 03:19:10.348
a city. I know I haven't been here, my tenure hasn't been that long,

03:19:10.461 --> 03:19:14.250
but with the volume of traffic we have, I just think it's— I wish

03:19:14.298 --> 03:19:17.301
we could keep them, but I don't think it services the city. And that's why

03:19:17.801 --> 03:19:21.682
we're trying to look to beautify the landscapes in other things. We have 40 to

03:19:22.182 --> 03:19:25.772
60 feet of landscape in Ranches Parkway, and so there's a lot of things to

03:19:26.272 --> 03:19:28.291
work with. And I'm working with our arborist to see how we can really spruce

03:19:28.339 --> 03:19:31.291
that, kind of bang that entrance up really well as you come down.

03:19:31.884 --> 03:19:35.494
Yeah, I mean, I, I— when you turn into UVU,

03:19:35.574 --> 03:19:38.477
I'll use that as an example, coming off the freeway, I mean,

03:19:38.525 --> 03:19:41.750
there's a nice median. It just makes— I think medians make it

03:19:42.250 --> 03:19:46.065
interesting, if you know what I mean. When you're driving down a road, it breaks

03:19:46.565 --> 03:19:50.227
up Just the monotony of just a flat asphalt surface, so that that's

03:19:50.985 --> 03:19:53.793
just one one thing to consider. And I'm not saying that we need to have

03:19:54.390 --> 03:19:57.784
grass; that's probably terrible to have out there. The way that we're watering the asphalt

03:19:58.284 --> 03:20:01.069
doesn't make any sense, but some basic drip lines for trees.

03:20:03.702 --> 03:20:07.383
I think for me as a resident, I it there's character at that

03:20:07.883 --> 03:20:10.277
part of our city, and I just it like makes me kind of sad just

03:20:10.777 --> 03:20:14.179
to see that completely go away. Isn't the high T— you're talking about

03:20:14.679 --> 03:20:18.161
just a barrier at all? High T, I'm good. I'm like 80%

03:20:18.434 --> 03:20:21.533
of the way there on that specific road. High T's, I'm good. Intersections,

03:20:22.111 --> 03:20:24.825
all of that probably needs to happen, even the widening of the lanes. Well,

03:20:24.857 --> 03:20:28.389
I'm saying the high T is the— there's a median, like you wouldn't be

03:20:28.889 --> 03:20:31.986
able to go across campus in the Maverick. There is a barrier.

03:20:32.146 --> 03:20:35.277
It's not a landscape. Right, but this— right, the high T,

03:20:35.293 --> 03:20:38.344
it's gotta go on both ends. Completely.

03:20:38.441 --> 03:20:41.903
I'm talking about where there's stretches of

03:20:42.403 --> 03:20:45.783
long span of road, just having something where you're not

03:20:45.896 --> 03:20:49.295
turning, it's just a span of road, keeping something

03:20:49.394 --> 03:20:51.347
in there to keep the road kind of interesting.

03:20:54.350 --> 03:20:57.684
In terms— in other words, because there's those some longer stretches where someone might

03:20:58.184 --> 03:21:01.679
not need the middle lane to do something, can you keep some kind of decorative

03:21:01.711 --> 03:21:04.903
feature? Some trees, shrubs, with some

03:21:04.953 --> 03:21:07.344
rocks in there, some desert. I don't know.

03:21:08.440 --> 03:21:11.874
Just, it's a question if we could retool that or redesign that a little bit.

03:21:17.356 --> 03:21:20.406
Turn your mic on, Dave. Thanks, guys. Even if we're not watering it, like,

03:21:20.422 --> 03:21:23.069
someone still has to go in and maintain that and clean that. I mean,

03:21:23.101 --> 03:21:27.280
so there is a safety component of that, especially if we're adding more lanes and

03:21:27.413 --> 03:21:30.536
more traffic. So I know from— if Zach were here, I know

03:21:31.036 --> 03:21:33.950
he'd be pulling on his beard right now as well. I get it.

03:21:34.095 --> 03:21:37.364
I totally get it. I'm here to represent

03:21:37.396 --> 03:21:41.388
the residents that live in that area, and I

03:21:41.437 --> 03:21:45.773
think it's worth at least considering the design and retaining

03:21:45.870 --> 03:21:49.484
some features in the middle there. So I'm going to throw

03:21:49.516 --> 03:21:52.712
out a quick question just to be sure before we spend time maybe doing that.

03:21:52.777 --> 03:21:56.161
Does council agree with that? I'm not sure. that there's a— I'll probably tell you,

03:21:56.177 --> 03:21:59.549
Mayor, that I'm, I'm not favorable to that. I mean, and look,

03:21:59.613 --> 03:22:02.825
I, I understand the sentiment, um, Councilmember Huish. For you,

03:22:02.857 --> 03:22:06.245
it's about the aesthetics and the way it feels. And I know Councilmember Wood has

03:22:06.745 --> 03:22:08.974
spoken about how that felt to him when he first came into the city.

03:22:09.039 --> 03:22:12.121
So I think that there's, there's a real impact

03:22:12.218 --> 03:22:15.349
there. But as, as I look at that and try to

03:22:15.429 --> 03:22:19.202
balance the maintenance aspect of it, the safety aspect of it for

03:22:19.702 --> 03:22:22.382
our employees who have to take care of it, the water in there and the

03:22:22.882 --> 03:22:25.768
additional road damage. I think that the suggestion,

03:22:26.462 --> 03:22:30.045
and this is at least what I think David was alluding to, is improving

03:22:30.158 --> 03:22:33.580
those sidescapes is what I'm going to call them, so the edges of the road,

03:22:34.095 --> 03:22:37.903
so that they take on that type of character. I think that's a

03:22:38.033 --> 03:22:41.261
good compromise. But if we're

03:22:41.278 --> 03:22:44.448
going to remove the medians, we're going to remove the medians. And every place that

03:22:44.948 --> 03:22:48.871
they've come out, They've come out, and we've while I've

03:22:48.887 --> 03:22:52.761
been here, we've made some improvements. And it is for me, it's too little

03:22:53.261 --> 03:22:57.783
too late. It's like when we did the left turn pocket on Pony

03:22:57.799 --> 03:23:01.390
Express Parkway, where you turn left onto ranches. We extended

03:23:01.423 --> 03:23:05.301
that 100 feet, and it was worthless because by the time that it

03:23:05.801 --> 03:23:09.081
was done, it was still backing up into the travel lane. I just think that

03:23:09.581 --> 03:23:11.410
we have a volume of traffic that we have to get in and. Out of

03:23:11.426 --> 03:23:14.975
the city, and that's a major thoroughfare for our city. And I know some of

03:23:15.475 --> 03:23:18.603
the residents there said, well, tell everybody in City Center to take Airport Road.

03:23:18.668 --> 03:23:22.329
We don't want them coming down Ranches. Well, I don't, I don't adopt that philosophy.

03:23:22.907 --> 03:23:26.359
I think citizens in the City Center ought to have the choice of where they

03:23:26.439 --> 03:23:31.417
want to exit the city, including Lake Mountain Corridor. So for,

03:23:31.497 --> 03:23:35.222
for me, I just, I, I think that, I think we're holding on to something

03:23:35.286 --> 03:23:38.901
that belongs in a smaller in a smaller road network.

03:23:39.945 --> 03:23:43.785
So I'll— I'm going to hit somewhere in the middle on this

03:23:44.285 --> 03:23:47.626
one. I agree with what you're saying, Brett. I think that it— that road needs

03:23:47.722 --> 03:23:50.615
to be without any barriers in the middle of it. I think we've seen enough

03:23:51.115 --> 03:23:54.873
accidents. Uh, it is an absolute blind spot coming out of any of

03:23:55.373 --> 03:23:58.922
those roads. However, I don't think we

03:23:59.422 --> 03:24:02.970
should go small when we do the landscaping on the side It— we're

03:24:03.002 --> 03:24:06.816
well known for that entrance into the city, and it should still

03:24:06.912 --> 03:24:10.694
present itself the same way when we do the sidescapes on the

03:24:11.194 --> 03:24:14.332
side. It should be incredibly beautiful the way that it is right now,

03:24:15.342 --> 03:24:18.627
in a way that can be presented on the side. So when we're looking at

03:24:19.127 --> 03:24:22.104
that, let's, let's put our best foot forward, uh,

03:24:22.296 --> 03:24:25.566
and make that area just as striking as it has always been,

03:24:25.982 --> 03:24:29.171
uh, for our city. It ought to look like a golf of course, Councilmember Wood,

03:24:29.267 --> 03:24:33.258
and, and sincerely, and it ought to have the impact of

03:24:33.770 --> 03:24:36.895
protecting those residents from that major roadway, from some of

03:24:37.395 --> 03:24:40.982
the noise, the lights, and some of those other impacts. I, I think that's

03:24:41.482 --> 03:24:44.170
where we can make a difference for the residents. And can I show a quick

03:24:44.670 --> 03:24:47.920
example? So this is Stonebridge, and so this is one great example because

03:24:47.952 --> 03:24:50.741
we have these great, you know, trees here in the middle, then we have this

03:24:50.757 --> 03:24:54.642
bare patch over here. And so we're looking at bringing in Trees,

03:24:54.674 --> 03:24:57.858
you know, landscaping this up, making it look really good. So we're kind of moving

03:24:57.874 --> 03:25:01.316
the green over, and any trees we can't relocate, we're going

03:25:01.348 --> 03:25:04.810
to reuse as mulch. So nothing's getting thrown away, everything's going to be repurposed throughout

03:25:05.310 --> 03:25:07.544
the city. We're going to try and relocate things, but it doesn't always work.

03:25:10.756 --> 03:25:14.057
So— I, I think I,

03:25:14.218 --> 03:25:17.777
I get the vision. Our track record,

03:25:17.793 --> 03:25:21.551
when I drive through City Center and I look at our True. Or even on

03:25:21.599 --> 03:25:24.803
Ranches Parkway now. It just, it just doesn't

03:25:24.820 --> 03:25:28.169
look good. And I think if we're going to pull that median out,

03:25:28.796 --> 03:25:32.058
it's got to look awesome. And I'm just going to give you that commission

03:25:33.953 --> 03:25:37.408
to really impress, to blow us away with the landscaping.

03:25:37.665 --> 03:25:40.743
I don't know whose job that is, but our track

03:25:40.808 --> 03:25:42.773
record throughout the city has not been great.

03:25:45.419 --> 03:25:49.357
As we mentioned previously, I mean, The ranches in the Pony Corridor,

03:25:49.966 --> 03:25:53.797
it's been an interesting one because a lot of the side streets,

03:25:53.893 --> 03:25:57.243
just like we've talked about in the city center, we've had a lot of fiber

03:25:57.743 --> 03:26:01.234
companies come through and they wreck irrigation systems.

03:26:01.490 --> 03:26:05.065
And I think our park staff has made a significant push

03:26:05.193 --> 03:26:08.847
in trying to get those irrigation systems operating again.

03:26:09.585 --> 03:26:13.432
And I feel like there's a little bit of an improvement. I think where

03:26:13.932 --> 03:26:16.624
when we take these medians out, we just need to plan Plant some more trees

03:26:16.656 --> 03:26:20.657
and shrubberies on the side of the road where it's feasible, because there's

03:26:21.157 --> 03:26:25.236
still going to be some narrow pockets that won't fit trees either. So we

03:26:25.736 --> 03:26:29.575
just need to lay it out in a way. But we're hoping that we're

03:26:30.057 --> 03:26:33.721
getting towards the end of these fiber companies destroying stuff when they come through

03:26:33.769 --> 03:26:36.918
multiple times every year, and it makes it

03:26:37.418 --> 03:26:41.257
hard to maintain. And I I couldn't agree more with.

03:26:41.386 --> 03:26:46.133
the medians, they, they need to come out. I, I think about Pioneer

03:26:46.633 --> 03:26:49.917
Crossing and they put the medians in there and how long did that last?

03:26:50.206 --> 03:26:53.253
And I just feel like if we just do a scale back,

03:26:53.269 --> 03:26:57.182
we're not going to meet the mark. I think we need to like meet

03:26:57.682 --> 03:27:00.855
the mark with it and let's attempt to do the sides and

03:27:01.047 --> 03:27:03.661
get it up to par so that it has that look and that feel that

03:27:03.773 --> 03:27:07.285
you're requesting. So I think I just want to

03:27:07.607 --> 03:27:10.788
Maybe stamp this one. Let's design it.

03:27:11.158 --> 03:27:14.677
Let's not dig holes and put trees in. Like, this needs to have an

03:27:15.177 --> 03:27:17.874
actual design, a landscaping design, to be brought to us to,

03:27:18.694 --> 03:27:21.988
to match what the citizens have become to expect in that area.

03:27:22.646 --> 03:27:25.844
And then let's set that as the standard of how we're going to go down

03:27:26.344 --> 03:27:29.523
our corridors so that it's not one area that's getting this, that this is something

03:27:29.556 --> 03:27:33.075
we're working for over the next 5 and 10 years so that

03:27:33.575 --> 03:27:36.635
our corridors are something that is reflective of how we feel about our city.

03:27:38.062 --> 03:27:41.589
Yeah, I was actually going to exactly recommend that thing. Get a landscape

03:27:42.089 --> 03:27:45.422
architect and let's do it right and design that,

03:27:45.518 --> 03:27:48.276
and then we can see what Pony could look like as well. And we got

03:27:48.776 --> 03:27:50.729
to put the funds into these areas. We just have to. And I think if

03:27:51.229 --> 03:27:53.455
we can show the residents that live in that area, hey, this is what it

03:27:53.955 --> 03:27:56.534
will become, they may say, we, we love this.

03:27:56.887 --> 03:27:59.869
It's gonna be safer, it's gonna be better, but it's also going to look more

03:28:00.369 --> 03:28:02.405
beautiful for them because that The trail is just going to go up right along

03:28:02.501 --> 03:28:06.114
where their homes are. I love that idea, but I think

03:28:06.614 --> 03:28:08.843
we got to put the money into it and actually make it real. It's the

03:28:09.343 --> 03:28:12.344
entrance, one of the entrances to the city. It's the first impression, so we got

03:28:12.844 --> 03:28:15.764
to make it great. 100%. We absolutely plan on doing that.

03:28:16.245 --> 03:28:19.409
And on Pony Express, what we're looking to do is later this year or

03:28:19.473 --> 03:28:23.696
even next fiscal year, we're looking to do a study on Pony Express to

03:28:24.196 --> 03:28:27.513
see what that looks like, what it looks like removing the medians, The landscape

03:28:28.013 --> 03:28:31.067
component, and something that was brought up in the last council or two councils ago

03:28:31.426 --> 03:28:35.444
was the monuments. Ranches and

03:28:35.944 --> 03:28:38.690
Pony Express have a lot of monuments, and that's been a big character of our

03:28:39.190 --> 03:28:42.147
city. We don't want to get rid of that. And when we widen, they're going

03:28:42.647 --> 03:28:44.701
to have to go where they are for the turn movements and the width.

03:28:45.058 --> 03:28:48.459
And so part of this project and the study on Pony Express is to look

03:28:48.959 --> 03:28:51.374
at where's the best place we can put these new monuments in so we can

03:28:51.874 --> 03:28:55.046
maintain that feel. for the city, but still have our turning movements and our travel

03:28:55.546 --> 03:28:59.033
lanes. Is the design what we're

03:28:59.533 --> 03:29:02.689
talking about built into that $11.4 million? Yes. With MAG?

03:29:03.078 --> 03:29:06.574
Yep. So it's $11.4 million. Golf course level design? Yep. Okay, David.

03:29:06.883 --> 03:29:10.538
It's a full design. It's a lot of contingency because we know

03:29:10.555 --> 03:29:14.304
MAG loves contingency. It's also money for construction

03:29:14.401 --> 03:29:17.795
and management. We usually— we handle management ourselves.

03:29:17.970 --> 03:29:21.216
throughout construction. This is a big enough project, we're going to hire out

03:29:21.716 --> 03:29:25.411
someone to help us with construction inspection management because it's a big project.

03:29:25.716 --> 03:29:28.713
And, you know, we'll have other things going on as well. So all those have

03:29:28.729 --> 03:29:32.052
been budgeted. I think it's

03:29:32.133 --> 03:29:35.908
important to mention, I know there's a lot of attention on the City Council

03:29:36.408 --> 03:29:39.635
right now because we're looking at different funding options for us as a city.

03:29:40.038 --> 03:29:43.396
And I think if somebody were to tune in, they'd be like, they want

03:29:43.896 --> 03:29:47.522
to spend their money on a golf Of course, landscaping and all of those things.

03:29:47.586 --> 03:29:51.802
And I think it's important to help people understand that one, this will be money

03:29:51.898 --> 03:29:55.215
that we get from the state through MEG. So it's

03:29:55.715 --> 03:29:59.543
not like we're wasting taxpayer dollars by doing something. And two,

03:30:00.152 --> 03:30:03.229
this has a big impact on property values and different things

03:30:03.729 --> 03:30:07.316
like that. And ultimately, we won't do it if we don't have

03:30:07.380 --> 03:30:10.650
the funds to back it up. And I think normally we

03:30:11.150 --> 03:30:13.807
wouldn't have to stop and say that, but I want residents to understand that we're

03:30:14.307 --> 03:30:17.888
not just like on a spending spree, that this is a part of a long

03:30:18.388 --> 03:30:22.146
process of finding the funding to go toward it. So that's

03:30:22.646 --> 03:30:26.586
it. So just ask

03:30:26.732 --> 03:30:30.283
clarifying questions again. So Phase 1 of Ranches Parkway would begin

03:30:31.192 --> 03:30:35.533
when approximately? And then, and then Phase 1 does not include widening,

03:30:36.515 --> 03:30:39.703
right? So, so there's nothing happening on the widening just yet on Phase 1?

03:30:40.574 --> 03:30:43.946
Correct. So for Phase 1, the project,

03:30:44.090 --> 03:30:47.607
if everything goes smoothly, next week, next Thursday, we, we'll be awarded this project.

03:30:47.880 --> 03:30:51.396
Funding will be secured with the MPO meeting. Then we would then

03:30:51.637 --> 03:30:54.896
work with, um, we have budgeted for fiscal year '27 to

03:30:55.250 --> 03:30:58.188
kind of cash flow this project because we know the road's already going to fail.

03:30:58.557 --> 03:31:01.833
The intersection's already failing down at the south end. And what we've done with a

03:31:01.913 --> 03:31:05.189
lot of MAG projects is the city has cash funded parts of it and then

03:31:05.446 --> 03:31:08.891
entered into an interlocal for reimbursement so we can get these projects done sooner

03:31:08.907 --> 03:31:12.320
than later than waiting until 2030. And so this is something,

03:31:12.737 --> 03:31:16.551
once next week it gets approved, I'm going to be then

03:31:16.583 --> 03:31:20.269
drafting an interlocal with Utah County and, um, UDOT to get

03:31:20.769 --> 03:31:23.874
this funding in place so we can begin design hopefully in the next month or

03:31:24.374 --> 03:31:28.265
two. So, David, and just to clarify this, when we typically,

03:31:28.297 --> 03:31:31.614
like Airport Road for instance, uh, MAG Funded that,

03:31:31.726 --> 03:31:35.556
we built it, and we're still waiting on some reimbursement. This Phase

03:31:35.652 --> 03:31:38.809
1, we do these improvements right away

03:31:39.309 --> 03:31:42.815
even though the funding— is it 2029? It's, it's '29 through '30.

03:31:42.880 --> 03:31:46.549
Yes. '30. So, so, but yeah, we start this project.

03:31:46.950 --> 03:31:50.588
Yep, right away. And, and the great thing is, because the city uses this

03:31:51.088 --> 03:31:54.610
approach, we actually saved money on this. We did Airport Road. I forget

03:31:54.642 --> 03:31:57.287
the dollar on Airport Road, but when we did the roundabout removal at Eagle Mountain

03:31:57.303 --> 03:32:00.886
Boulevard and Pony Express. That was a $3.6 million project

03:32:01.480 --> 03:32:04.871
with, uh, it was MAG funded, and we saved, I think

03:32:05.371 --> 03:32:08.711
it was $800,000 we left over. And they're allowing us to use that to continue

03:32:09.211 --> 03:32:12.150
some of the widening further south because we cash funded that.

03:32:12.407 --> 03:32:15.701
Because from this, this diagram here, you know,

03:32:15.765 --> 03:32:18.962
the project was estimated $11.8 million current dollars,

03:32:19.541 --> 03:32:23.476
and they budget for 2030 funds, which is, you know, $13.8 million. So So

03:32:23.976 --> 03:32:26.660
if we can do it early, it's kudos to us. Like us,

03:32:26.886 --> 03:32:30.312
Saratoga and Lehigh are some of the few cities that cash flow and do these

03:32:30.361 --> 03:32:33.450
early. Most other cities, they're late. They come back asking

03:32:33.482 --> 03:32:36.777
for extra money. So like we're ahead of the game and we're going to— that's

03:32:37.277 --> 03:32:39.879
the way we can save our residents money and use those funds to better purpose.

03:32:41.731 --> 03:32:45.500
So we've spent a lot of time on specifically ranches, but it's the entire TMP

03:32:46.000 --> 03:32:49.274
that we're looking at here. I just wanted to make sure that especially Councilmember

03:32:49.290 --> 03:32:52.522
Huish is comfortable with— at least I heard 2 opinions

03:32:53.022 --> 03:32:56.337
on that removal of the roundabout or medians.

03:32:57.642 --> 03:33:01.064
Are you comfortable if we move on without? Yeah, we can move forward.

03:33:01.433 --> 03:33:05.193
Okay. I know you wanted to go with some design elements

03:33:05.693 --> 03:33:08.553
and I didn't want to direct staff to do that if there wasn't an appetite.

03:33:10.459 --> 03:33:11.927
So I'm talking about the median specifically.

03:33:17.230 --> 03:33:20.640
Isn't that part of the— No, so Councilmember Herwish was saying he wanted to leave

03:33:21.140 --> 03:33:23.180
